  Capture CMD every time you run it: its output is shown as always and a copy
  is filed (as 'provost run --show CMD' would). Each command becomes a shell
  function in one file, which your shell loads from its startup file; provost
  says how. With no CMD, list the commands listened on.
EOF
            ;;
        unlisten) echo "usage: provost unlisten CMD...    stop capturing CMD every time it runs" ;;
        search) cat <<'EOF'
usage: provost search PATTERN [NAME...] [options]
  Find the dataset rows that match PATTERN (a regular expression, matched
  anywhere in the row, '#' and capture included) with git grep, shown as a
  table per dataset. NAME... limits it to those datasets.
options:
  -i, -F, -w              ignore case / a fixed string / whole words
  --at ID                 search the store as it stood after capture ID
  --output                search the captured outputs instead (capture, line, text)
  --files                 search a controlled directory's files as the latest
                          run kept them (or capture ID's, with --at)
EOF
            ;;
        diff) cat <<'EOF'
usage: provost diff [NAME] [A [B]] [-- PATH...]
  What changed between capture A and capture B: for each dataset, the rows
  only A filed (-) and only B filed (+), compared without the columns
  provost adds; and, in a controlled directory, which files changed. With
  NAME, only that dataset, and the default captures are its own.
  No A: the last two captures; A alone: A against the capture before it.
  -- PATH...              the full diff of those files between A and B
EOF
            ;;
        next) echo "usage: provost next    step the restored store forward one capture (past the last: back to the latest)" ;;
        prev) echo "usage: provost prev    step the store back one capture (from the latest: the last capture)" ;;
        ls) echo "usage: provost ls    list datasets" ;;
        show) cat <<'EOF'
usage: provost show NAME [--capture ID] [--at ID] [--tsv]
  Present a dataset as a table: '#' is each row's index in the store,
  'capture' the capture it came from, 'n' its record number in that capture.
options:
  --capture ID            only the rows capture ID filed (c0007, or 7)
  --at ID                 the dataset as it stood just after capture ID, read
                          from git (the store stays where it is)
  --tsv                   tab-separated, as stored, for a program to read: a
                          blank cell stays blank, and no matching rows print
                          the header alone (Python: provost_data.load)
EOF
            ;;
        rm) cat <<'EOF'
usage: provost rm [-f|--yes] [--no-purge] NAME...    delete dataset(s) + the captures only they used
       provost rm [-f|--yes] --capture ID[,ID...]...
  Delete datasets, and the captures that fed only them (--no-purge: keep
  those). Or, with --capture, delete captures and the rows they filed, from
  every dataset they fed; a dataset left with no rows goes too. Either way
  the deletion is one commit, and git's history keeps what was there.
EOF
            ;;
        annotate) cat <<'EOF'
usage: provost annotate ID KEY=VALUE...
  Add run-manifest keys decided after the run (reviewer, disposition, ...) to
  capture ID (c0007) without filing it again: they are kept beside it as
  captures/ID/annotations.txt, setting a key again replaces it, and the
  change is a commit of its own. A value may hold spaces (quote it for the
  shell). 'provost source ID' shows them; 'provost check-manifest' counts them.
EOF
            ;;
        check-manifest) cat <<'EOF'
usage: provost check-manifest [--profile P] [--post] [--strict] [ID|NAME...]
  Check each capture's run manifest -- its '#%' prologue and its annotations
  -- against a profile, and list the required keys each one lacks or leaves
  empty. No ID: every capture in the store; a dataset NAME: the captures that
  fed it.
options:
  --profile P             the profile: a NAME (default, proposal, or one in
                          ~/.config/provost/manifests) or a .tsv file
                          (default: PROVOST_MANIFEST_PROFILE, else the store's
                          manifest-profile.tsv, else 'default')
  --post                  check the post-run keys (reviewer, disposition) too
  --strict                exit 1 if any capture is incomplete (default: 0)
EOF
            ;;
        stats) echo "usage: provost stats NAME [analyze-args...]    statistics on a dataset (--all for everything)" ;;
        triage) echo "usage: provost triage    show the held-back bucket (unmatched, low-confidence, failed quality gate), raw input decoded" ;;
        doctor) echo "usage: provost doctor    check the engines and the store; lists failed background captures" ;;
        verify) cat <<'EOF'
usage: provost verify [--capture ID[,ID...]]...
  Check the store's integrity: git fsck --full; no tracked file changed and
  left uncommitted; every capture-* tag names a commit on the store's history
  (and every files-* tag a commit); each capture's output.txt is the one its tag filed and re-hashes
  to the sha256 in its meta.tsv and its commit's Output-SHA256 trailer; and
  capture ids have no gap the history cannot explain (an id 'rm' removed is
  counted, not failed). Exit 0 with no findings, 1 with a list of them.
  Captures filed before provost recorded hashes (2.3.0 and earlier) are
  counted as unhashed, not failed.
options:
  --capture ID            check only these captures' tags and hashes (fsck
                          and the id sequence still cover the whole store)
what it proves:
  That the store is internally consistent: nothing was edited, lost or
  dropped without rewriting the history to match. It does NOT prove that the
  history was never rewritten: anyone who can write the store can rebuild
  every commit, hash and tag. To anchor it, record the head commit verify
  prints (and the store's capture count) somewhere the store's writers
  cannot change -- a test report, a ticket -- and check it later.
EOF
            ;;
        *) return 1 ;;
    esac
}
_cmd_help () {
    _usage_text "$1" | sed -e "s/\\\$PROVOST_MIN_CONFIDENCE/$PROVOST_MIN_CONFIDENCE/" \
        -e "s/\\\$PROVOST_MIN_COVERAGE/$PROVOST_MIN_COVERAGE/" -e "s/\\\$PROVOST_MIN_CONSISTENCY/$PROVOST_MIN_CONSISTENCY/"
    exit 0
}
_cmd_usage_die () { local u; u="$(_usage_text "$1")"; printf '%s\n' "${u%%$'\n'*}" >&2; exit 2; }

# Parse the options shared by inject and run. Sets: _O_THRESHOLD _O_ASYNC _O_NAME
# and leaves the remaining args in _O_REST.
# _capture_opt VERB ARGS... — apply the one capture option at the front of
# ARGS, and set _CO_N to how many words it took (0: it is not one). Shared by
# _parse_capture_opts (inject, run) and cmd_batch, so the three take the same
# options with the same checks.
_capture_opt () {
    local verb="$1"; shift
    _CO_N=1
    case "$1" in
        -q|--silent) PROVOST_QUIET=1 ;;
        --min-confidence)
            [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--min-confidence needs a number."
            _validate_uint --min-confidence "$2" 0 101; _O_THRESHOLD="$(_norm_uint "$2")"; _CO_N=2 ;;
        --min-confidence=*)
            _validate_uint --min-confidence "${1#*=}" 0 101; _O_THRESHOLD="$(_norm_uint "${1#*=}")" ;;
        --min-coverage|--min-consistency)
            [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "$1 needs a number."
            _validate_uint "$1" "$2" 0 100
            if [[ "$1" == --min-coverage ]]; then PROVOST_MIN_COVERAGE="$(_norm_uint "$2")"
            else PROVOST_MIN_CONSISTENCY="$(_norm_uint "$2")"; fi
            _CO_N=2 ;;
        --min-coverage=*)
            _validate_uint --min-coverage "${1#*=}" 0 100; PROVOST_MIN_COVERAGE="$(_norm_uint "${1#*=}")" ;;
        --min-consistency=*)
            _validate_uint --min-consistency "${1#*=}" 0 100; PROVOST_MIN_CONSISTENCY="$(_norm_uint "${1#*=}")" ;;
        --plugin-dir)
            [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--plugin-dir needs a directory."
            PROVOST_PLUGIN_DIR="$2"; _CO_N=2 ;;
        --name)
            [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--name needs a dataset name."
            _O_NAME="$2"; _CO_N=2 ;;
        --name=*) _O_NAME="${1#*=}" ;;
        --multi) PROVOST_MULTI=1 ;;
        --auto-multi) PROVOST_AUTO_MULTI=1 ;;
        --no-auto-multi) PROVOST_AUTO_MULTI=0 ;;
        --model) PROVOST_MODEL=1 ;;
        --no-model) PROVOST_MODEL=0 ;;
        --llm) PROVOST_LLM=1 ;;
        --no-llm) PROVOST_LLM=0 ;;
        --context-columns) PROVOST_CONTEXT_COLUMNS=1 ;;
        --no-context-columns) PROVOST_CONTEXT_COLUMNS=0 ;;
        --units)
            [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--units needs a mode: base or off."
            _units_mode "$2"; _CO_N=2 ;;
        --units=*) _units_mode "${1#*=}" ;;
        --redact) PROVOST_REDACT=1 ;;
        --no-redact) PROVOST_REDACT=0 ;;
        --manifest)
            [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--manifest needs a file of KEY=VALUE lines."
            _O_MANIFEST="$2"; _CO_N=2 ;;
        --manifest=*) _O_MANIFEST="${1#*=}" ;;
        --manifest-profile)
            [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--manifest-profile needs a profile NAME or file."
            _O_MPROFILE="$2"; _CO_N=2 ;;
        --manifest-profile=*) _O_MPROFILE="${1#*=}" ;;
        --strict-manifest) PROVOST_MANIFEST_STRICT=1 ;;
        --no-strict-manifest) PROVOST_MANIFEST_STRICT=0 ;;
        --env) PROVOST_ENV=1 ;;
        --env=modules) PROVOST_ENV=modules ;;
        --env=*) usage_die "--env takes no value, or 'modules' (--env=modules); got '$1'." ;;
        --no-env) PROVOST_ENV=0 ;;
        --async) _O_ASYNC=1 ;;
        --sync|--wait) _O_ASYNC=0 ;;
        --show|--tee) _O_SHOW=1 ;;
        -h|--help) _cmd_help "$verb" ;;
        *) _CO_N=0 ;;
    esac
    return 0
}

# --units MODE: base (a NAME[UNIT] column in base units beside each column
# whose unit can be read; transform/units.sh) or off (the default).
_units_mode () {
    case "$1" in
        base|off) PROVOST_UNITS="$1" ;;
        *) usage_die "--units must be base or off (got '$1')." ;;
    esac
}

_parse_capture_opts () {
    local verb="$1"; shift
    _O_THRESHOLD="$PROVOST_MIN_CONFIDENCE"; _O_ASYNC=""; _O_NAME=""; _O_SHOW=0; _O_REST=()
    _O_MANIFEST=""; _O_MPROFILE=""
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            --) shift; _O_REST=("$@"); return 0 ;;
            -?*)
                _capture_opt "$verb" "$@"
                (( _CO_N > 0 )) || usage_die "unknown option for '$verb': $1 (see 'provost $verb --help')."
                shift "$_CO_N" ;;
            *) _O_REST=("$@"); return 0 ;;
        esac
    done
    return 0
}
_check_capture_opts () {
    if [[ -n "$_O_NAME" ]]; then
        _require_name "$_O_NAME"
        [[ "$_O_NAME" == unsorted ]] && usage_die "'unsorted' is reserved for the triage bucket; pick another --name."
    fi
    if [[ -n "${PROVOST_PLUGIN_DIR:-}" && ! -d "$PROVOST_PLUGIN_DIR" ]]; then
        die "plugin strategy dir not found: $PROVOST_PLUGIN_DIR"
    fi
    _manifest_capture_setup
    return 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default verb: inject (pipe or file)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cmd_inject () {
    _parse_capture_opts inject "$@"
    local file=""
    [[ ${#_O_REST[@]} -le 1 ]] || usage_die "inject takes at most one FILE (got: ${_O_REST[*]})."
    [[ ${#_O_REST[@]} -eq 1 ]] && file="${_O_REST[0]}"
    _check_capture_opts
    if [[ -z "$file" && -t 0 ]]; then
        usage_die "no input — pipe data into provost (cmd | provost) or give a FILE; see 'provost help'."
    fi
    if [[ -n "$file" ]]; then [[ -f "$file" ]] || die "no such file: $file"; fi

    _ensure_workspace
    _require_on_branch
    _mktempd provost; local tmp="$_MKTEMPD"
    local in="$tmp/in.txt" size
    if [[ -n "$file" ]]; then
        head -c "$((PROVOST_MAX_INPUT + 1))" < "$file" > "$in.raw"
        _receive "$in.raw" "$in"
        (( _O_SHOW )) && cat -- "$file"
    elif (( _O_SHOW )); then
        # --show: pass all of stdin through to stdout (a tee), keep the bound.
        tee "$tmp/full"
        head -c "$((PROVOST_MAX_INPUT + 1))" "$tmp/full" > "$in.raw"; rm -f "$tmp/full"
        _receive "$in.raw" "$in"
    else
        head -c "$((PROVOST_MAX_INPUT + 1))" > "$in.raw"
        _receive "$in.raw" "$in"
    fi
    [[ -s "$in" ]] || die "no input to provost (empty)."
    size="$(wc -c < "$in")"; size="${size//[!0-9]/}"
    (( size <= PROVOST_MAX_INPUT )) \
        || die "input too large (max $PROVOST_MAX_INPUT bytes; raise PROVOST_MAX_INPUT)."
    _manifest_apply "$in" "${file:-input}" || exit 1

    if _want_async "$_O_ASYNC"; then _ingest_async "$in" "$_O_THRESHOLD" "inject" "" "" "$_O_NAME"
    else _ingest "$in" "$_O_THRESHOLD" "inject" "" "" "$_O_NAME"; fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# run — execute a command, capture its output as a commit, and ingest it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cmd_run () {
    _parse_capture_opts run "$@"
    set -- ${_O_REST[@]+"${_O_REST[@]}"}
    [[ $# -gt 0 ]] || { _cmd_usage_die run; }
    _check_capture_opts
    command -v -- "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { printf 'provost: command not found: %s\n' "$1" >&2; exit 127; }

    _ensure_workspace
    _require_on_branch
    _mktempd provost; local tmp="$_MKTEMPD"
    local in="$tmp/in.txt" rc=0 size
    # Capture combined stdout+stderr (the thing you'd otherwise eyeball),
    # bounded by PROVOST_MAX_INPUT exactly as inject is.
    local -a st=()
    set +e
    if (( _O_SHOW )); then
        # --show: the output goes to the terminal as it comes, as well.
        "$@" 2>&1 | tee "$in.full"
        st=("${PIPESTATUS[@]}")
        head -c "$((PROVOST_MAX_INPUT + 1))" "$in.full" > "$in.raw"; rm -f "$in.full"
    else
        "$@" 2>&1 | head -c "$((PROVOST_MAX_INPUT + 1))" > "$in.raw"
        st=("${PIPESTATUS[@]}")
    fi
    set -e
    rc="${st[0]}"
    size="$(wc -c < "$in.raw")"; size="${size//[!0-9]/}"
    if (( size > PROVOST_MAX_INPUT )); then
        die "command output too large (more than $PROVOST_MAX_INPUT bytes; raise PROVOST_MAX_INPUT) — nothing captured."
    fi
    _receive "$in.raw" "$in"
    # The command as a shell-quoted, TSV-safe string (printf %q never emits a
    # raw tab or newline), so meta.tsv and 'provost log' show it faithfully.
    local cmd; cmd="$(_shell_quote "$@")"
    # ...with its secrets scrubbed too (mysql --password=..., curl -H 'Authorization: ...').
    if [[ "$PROVOST_REDACT" == 1 ]]; then cmd="$(_scrub_text "$cmd" "$in.redacted")"; fi
    [[ -s "$in" ]] || { info "command produced no output (rc=$rc) — nothing captured."; return "$rc"; }
    local name="$_O_NAME"
    [[ -n "$name" ]] || name="$(_run_name "$@")"
    _manifest_apply "$in" "$1" || exit 1
    if _want_async "$_O_ASYNC"; then _ingest_async "$in" "$_O_THRESHOLD" "run" "$cmd" "$rc" "$name"
    else _ingest "$in" "$_O_THRESHOLD" "run" "$cmd" "$rc" "$name"; fi
    return "$rc"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# batch — many inputs, one commit. Each input is a capture of its own (its own
# id, its own `provost source`), the parse halves run in parallel, and the
# store halves run in input order under one lock, ending in ONE commit with a
# tag per capture. For wrappers that capture a fleet: one input per host,
# with `#% host=...` context and --context-columns.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_ncpu () {
    local n; n="$(getconf _NPROCESSORS_ONLN 2>/dev/null || nproc 2>/dev/null || echo 4)"
    [[ "$n" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && (( n >= 1 )) || n=4
    (( n > 16 )) && n=16
    echo "$n"
}

# _batch_hint WORKDIR — a parse's hint for the rest of its batch group, on
# stdout (strategy, coverage, the table's header line), or nothing and
# status 1 when it was not one clean table: held, segmented, the model's or
# a template's, or with coverage unmeasured.
_batch_hint () {
    local d="$1"
    (
        source "$d/state.sh"
        [[ -z "$reason" && -z "$am_reason" && -z "$lm_reason" && -z "$llm_reason" ]] || exit 1
        [[ ${#ds_file[@]} -eq 1 && -n "$strategy" && "$cov" =~ ^[0-9]{1,3}$ ]] || exit 1
        [[ "$strategy" != layout-model && "$strategy" != llm-template ]] || exit 1
        [[ -s "$d/wide.tsv" ]] || exit 1
        printf '%s\n%s\n%s\n' "$strategy" "$cov" "$(head -n 1 "$d/wide.tsv")"
    )
}

# _batch_first_done — cmd_batch's: wait for the next first parse it started
# (pre_pid[ph], in launch order), keep its exit status, and the hint it
# gives the rest of its name when it read cleanly. Works on cmd_batch's
# locals, which bash lets a function it calls see.
_batch_first_done () {
    local g="${pre_g[ph]}" k i d rc=0
    k="${first_of[$g]}"; i="${todo[k]}"; d="$broot/$i"
    wait "${pre_pid[ph]}" || rc=$?
    pre_rc[$k]=$rc
    if (( rc == 0 )) && _batch_hint "$d" > "$broot/hint.$k"; then
        hint_of[$g]="$broot/hint.$k"
    fi
    ph=$((ph + 1))
}

cmd_batch () {
    _O_THRESHOLD="$PROVOST_MIN_CONFIDENCE"; _O_ASYNC=""; _O_NAME=""; _O_SHOW=0
    _O_MANIFEST=""; _O_MPROFILE=""
    local jobs="" line lname lpath
    local -a files=() names=()
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            -j|--jobs)
                [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--jobs needs a number."
                _validate_uint --jobs "$2" 1 1024; jobs="$(_norm_uint "$2")"; shift 2 ;;
            --jobs=*)
                _validate_uint --jobs "${1#*=}" 1 1024; jobs="$(_norm_uint "${1#*=}")"; shift ;;
            --from)
                # A list of inputs, one a line: PATH, or NAME<TAB>PATH for an
                # input of its own name; '-' reads the list from stdin. For
                # more inputs than a command line holds.
                [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--from needs a file (or - for stdin)."
                [[ "$2" == - || -f "$2" ]] || die "no such list: $2"
                while IFS= read -r line || [[ -n "$line" ]]; do
                    line="${line%$'\r'}"
                    [[ -z "$line" || "$line" == '#'* ]] && continue
                    if [[ "$line" == *$'\t'* ]]; then lname="${line%%$'\t'*}"; lpath="${line#*$'\t'}"
                    else lname="$_O_NAME"; lpath="$line"; fi
                    files+=("$lpath"); names+=("$lname")
                done < <(if [[ "$2" == - ]]; then cat; else cat -- "$2"; fi)
                shift 2 ;;
            --) shift
                while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do files+=("$1"); names+=("$_O_NAME"); shift; done ;;
            -?*)
                _capture_opt batch "$@"
                (( _CO_N > 0 )) || usage_die "unknown option for 'batch': $1 (see 'provost batch --help')."
                shift "$_CO_N" ;;
            *) files+=("$1"); names+=("$_O_NAME"); shift ;;
        esac
    done
    [[ -z "$_O_ASYNC" && "$_O_SHOW" == 0 ]] \
        || usage_die "batch files in the foreground and shows nothing: --async, --sync and --show do not apply."
    (( ${#files[@]} > 0 )) || _cmd_usage_die batch
    local i missing=()
    for (( i = 0; i < ${#files[@]}; i++ )); do
        [[ -f "${files[i]}" ]] || missing+=("${files[i]}")
        if [[ -n "${names[i]}" ]]; then
            _require_name "${names[i]}"
            [[ "${names[i]}" == unsorted ]] && usage_die "'unsorted' is reserved for the triage bucket; pick another --name."
        fi
    done
    (( ${#missing[@]} == 0 )) || die "no such file: ${missing[*]} -- nothing was captured."
    if [[ -n "${PROVOST_PLUGIN_DIR:-}" && ! -d "$PROVOST_PLUGIN_DIR" ]]; then
        die "plugin strategy dir not found: $PROVOST_PLUGIN_DIR"
    fi
    _manifest_capture_setup
    [[ -n "$jobs" ]] || jobs="$(_ncpu)"
    # --llm may have Claude write a template from the first input that needs
    # one; parsed in parallel, every input would ask for the same template.
    if [[ "$PROVOST_LLM" == 1 && "$jobs" -gt 1 ]]; then
        info "batch: --llm parses one input at a time, so a template is written once, not once per input."
        jobs=1
    fi

    _ensure_workspace
    _require_on_branch
    _mktempd batch; local broot="$_MKTEMPD" d size failed=0 skipped=0
    local -a todo=()
    # Stage each input as inject does: bounded by PROVOST_MAX_INPUT, CRs
    # stripped. An empty one is said and skipped, as `run` skips a command
    # that printed nothing; an oversized one is an error for that input.
    for (( i = 0; i < ${#files[@]}; i++ )); do
        d="$broot/$i"; mkdir -p "$d"
        head -c "$((PROVOST_MAX_INPUT + 1))" < "${files[i]}" > "$d/in.raw"
        _receive "$d/in.raw" "$d/in.txt"
        if [[ ! -s "$d/in.txt" ]]; then
            info "${files[i]}: empty -- nothing captured."; skipped=$((skipped + 1)); continue
        fi
        size="$(wc -c < "$d/in.txt")"; size="${size//[!0-9]/}"
        if (( size > PROVOST_MAX_INPUT )); then
            warn "${files[i]}: too large (max $PROVOST_MAX_INPUT bytes; raise PROVOST_MAX_INPUT) -- not captured."
            failed=$((failed + 1)); continue
        fi
        if ! _manifest_apply "$d/in.txt" "${files[i]}"; then failed=$((failed + 1)); continue; fi
        todo+=("$i")
    done

    # DETECTION REUSE. The inputs of one name are usually one command's output
    # from many hosts: the same format, and detection -- trying every
    # strategy -- is most of the engine's time on a small input. The first
    # input of each name with others after it is parsed on its own; when it
    # is read cleanly (one table, nothing held, no auto-multi, model or
    # template), its strategy, header and coverage are the hint the others
    # try first (see _ingest_parse), each kept only if it is the same table.
    # Off with PROVOST_BATCH_REUSE=0, and under --multi, --model and --llm,
    # which read an input their own ways.
    local -A first_of=() group_n=() hint_of=() pre_rc=()
    local g
    if [[ "${PROVOST_BATCH_REUSE:-1}" == 1 && "$PROVOST_MULTI" != 1 \
          && "$PROVOST_MODEL" != 1 && "$PROVOST_LLM" != 1 ]]; then
        for (( k = 0; k < ${#todo[@]}; k++ )); do
            g="=${names[${todo[k]}]}"
            group_n[$g]=$(( ${group_n[$g]:-0} + 1 ))
            [[ -n "${first_of[$g]+x}" ]] || first_of[$g]=$k
        done
        # The first inputs are parsed side by side, at most $jobs at a time,
        # as the rest are below: one after another, a batch of many names (a
        # fleet's answers to several commands at once) waited on each first
        # parse in turn with the other CPUs idle.
        local -a pre_g=() pre_pid=()
        local ph=0
        for g in "${!first_of[@]}"; do
            (( ${group_n[$g]} > 1 )) || continue
            k="${first_of[$g]}"; i="${todo[k]}"; d="$broot/$i"
            (
                _TMPDIRS=(); _LOCKS_HELD=(); _LOCKS_OWNER=()
                trap _cleanup EXIT
                _ingest_parse "$d/in.txt" "$_O_THRESHOLD" "" "${names[i]}" "$d"
            ) > "$d/parse.log" 2>&1 &
            pre_g+=("$g"); pre_pid+=("$!")
            while (( ${#pre_pid[@]} - ph >= jobs )); do
                _batch_first_done
            done
        done
        while (( ph < ${#pre_pid[@]} )); do
            _batch_first_done
        done
    fi

    # The parse halves, at most $jobs at a time. Each child starts with no
    # temp dirs or locks of the parent's to clean up, as _ingest_async's does:
    # its EXIT trap must never remove the batch's own work dirs.
    local -a pids=() parsed=()
    local k head=0 rc
    for (( k = 0; k < ${#todo[@]}; k++ )); do
        i="${todo[k]}"; d="$broot/$i"
        if [[ -n "${pre_rc[$k]+x}" ]]; then
            pids[k]=""
        else
            (
                _TMPDIRS=(); _LOCKS_HELD=(); _LOCKS_OWNER=()
                trap _cleanup EXIT
                _BATCH_HINT="${hint_of[=${names[i]}]:-}" \
                    _ingest_parse "$d/in.txt" "$_O_THRESHOLD" "" "${names[i]}" "$d"
            ) > "$d/parse.log" 2>&1 &
            pids[k]=$!
        fi
        while (( k + 1 - head >= jobs )); do
            rc=0
            if [[ -n "${pids[head]}" ]]; then wait "${pids[head]}" || rc=$?
            else rc="${pre_rc[$head]}"; fi
            if (( rc == 0 )); then parsed+=("${todo[head]}")
            else
                warn "${files[${todo[head]}]}: not captured: $(awk 'NF { l = $0 } END { print l }' "$broot/${todo[head]}/parse.log")"
                failed=$((failed + 1))
            fi
            head=$((head + 1))
        done
    done
    while (( head < ${#todo[@]} )); do
        rc=0
        if [[ -n "${pids[head]}" ]]; then wait "${pids[head]}" || rc=$?
        else rc="${pre_rc[$head]}"; fi
        if (( rc == 0 )); then parsed+=("${todo[head]}")
        else
            warn "${files[${todo[head]}]}: not captured: $(awk 'NF { l = $0 } END { print l }' "$broot/${todo[head]}/parse.log")"
            failed=$((failed + 1))
        fi
        head=$((head + 1))
    done
    # (waited in launch order, so parsed[] is in input order: capture ids are
    # given out in the order the inputs were named, whatever finished first)

    if (( ${#parsed[@]} > 0 )); then
        _lock
        _require_on_branch
        local -a ids=()
        local rows0 rows1
        rows0="$(cat "$(_rowid_file)" 2>/dev/null || echo 1)"
        for i in "${parsed[@]}"; do
            _ingest_store "$broot/$i/in.txt" batch "" "" "$broot/$i" 0
            ids+=("$_CAPID")
            printf 'input\t%s\n' "$(_meta_safe "${files[i]}")" >> "$(_cap_dir)/$_CAPID/meta.tsv"
        done
        rows1="$(cat "$(_rowid_file)" 2>/dev/null || echo 1)"
        local first="${ids[0]}" last="${ids[${#ids[@]}-1]}" id
        for id in "${ids[@]}"; do
            printf 'batch\t%s..%s\n' "$first" "$last" >> "$(_cap_dir)/$id/meta.tsv"
        done
        local ctl_dir=""
        if [[ "${PROVOST_STORE_KIND:-}" == controlled:* ]]; then
            ctl_dir="$(dirname "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE")"
            _snapshot_context "$ctl_dir" "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE" >/dev/null \
                || warn "could not snapshot the controlled directory's context."
        fi
        local range="$first..$last"; (( ${#ids[@]} == 1 )) && range="$first"
        _commit "batch: ${#ids[@]} capture(s) $range ($((rows1 - rows0)) rows)$(_hash_trailers "${ids[@]}")"
        local out
        for id in "${ids[@]}"; do
            out="$(_git tag "capture-$id" 2>&1)" || { printf 'git: %s\n' "$out" >&2; die "batch $first..$last was committed but $id could not be tagged capture-$id."; }
            if [[ -n "$ctl_dir" ]]; then
                _snapshot_files "$ctl_dir" "$id" \
                    || warn "could not save the controlled directory's files for $id (the capture itself is filed)."
            fi
        done
        _unlock
        local what="captures $first..$last"; (( ${#ids[@]} == 1 )) && what="capture $first"
        ok "batch: ${#ids[@]} input(s) -> $what, one commit$( (( skipped )) && printf '; %d empty' "$skipped")$( (( failed )) && printf '; %d not captured' "$failed")."
    else
        info "batch: nothing captured ($skipped empty, $failed not captured)."
    fi
    rm -rf "$broot"
    (( failed == 0 ))
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# log / restore — the git core: list captures, bring one back up.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cmd_log () {
    local failed=0
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            --failed) failed=1; shift ;;
            -h|--help) _cmd_help log ;;
            *) usage_die "unknown argument for 'log': $1 (see 'provost log --help')." ;;
        esac
    done
    if [[ $failed -eq 1 ]]; then _show_failures; return 0; fi
    local capdir cur=""; capdir="$(_cap_dir)"
    # Restored to an earlier capture: the store's files stop at that capture,
    # so list every capture from the latest's tree, read from git.
    if [[ -d "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.git" ]] && cur="$(_current_capture)" && [[ -n "$cur" ]]; then
        _mktempd log
        _git archive "$(_main_branch)" -- 'captures/*/meta.tsv' 2>/dev/null | tar -x -C "$_MKTEMPD" 2>/dev/null || true
        capdir="$_MKTEMPD/captures"
    fi
    local d b keys=() files=() k
    shopt -s nullglob
    for d in "$capdir"/c*/; do
        [[ -f "${d}meta.tsv" ]] || continue
        b="${d%/}"; b="${b##*/}"
        if [[ "$b" =~ ^c([0-9]{1,12})$ ]]; then keys+=("$((10#${BASH_REMATCH[1]}))"$'\t'"$b"); fi
    done
    shopt -u nullglob
    if [[ ${#keys[@]} -eq 0 ]]; then
        info "no captures yet — run 'provost run <cmd>' or pipe into 'provost'."; return 0
    fi
    # Numeric order (c10000 after c9999), each dir's real name kept.
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r k b; do
        files+=("$capdir/$b/meta.tsv")
    done < <(printf '%s\n' "${keys[@]}" | sort -n -k1,1)
    printf '%-6s %-20s %-7s %-14s %-12s %s\n' "ID" "WHEN" "SOURCE" "STRATEGY" "DATASET" "COMMAND" >&2
    # One awk over every meta.tsv (not six per capture).
    awk -F'\t' '
        function emit() {
            if (!have) return
            printf "%-6s %-20s %-7s %-14s %-12s %s\n", v["id"], v["ts"], v["source"], v["strategy"], v["dataset"], v["command"]
            split("", v); have = 0
        }
        FNR == 1 { emit() }
        { k = $1; val = substr($0, length(k) + 2); v[k] = val; have = 1 }
        END { emit() }' "${files[@]}"
    [[ -z "$cur" ]] || info "the store is restored to $cur — step with: provost next / prev; return with: provost restore latest"
}

cmd_restore () {
    [[ $# -ge 1 ]] || _cmd_usage_die restore
    case "$1" in -h|--help) _cmd_help restore ;; esac
    [[ $# -eq 1 ]] || _cmd_usage_die restore
    _ensure_workspace
    local target="$1" out main
    if [[ "$target" == "latest" ]]; then
        main="$(_main_branch)"
        _lock
        out="$(_git checkout -q "$main" 2>&1)" || { printf 'git: %s\n' "$out" >&2; die "could not return to latest ($main)."; }
        _unlock
        ok "restored workspace to latest ($main)."
        return 0
    fi
    target="$(_capid_of "$target")" || usage_die "invalid capture id '$1' (expected e.g. c0003, 3 or latest)."
    _git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/capture-$target" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
        || die "no such capture: $target (see 'provost log')."
    # Bring that capture's committed tree back up (detached).
    _lock
    out="$(_git checkout -q "capture-$target" 2>&1)" || { printf 'git: %s\n' "$out" >&2; die "checkout failed for capture-$target."; }
    _unlock
    ok "brought up $target (detached; new captures are refused until you return). Step with: provost next / prev; return with: provost restore latest"
    local capd; capd="$(_cap_dir)/$target"
    if [[ -f "$capd/meta.tsv" ]]; then echo; sed 's/\t/: /' "$capd/meta.tsv"; fi
}

# Every capture id, oldest first (from the capture-<id> tags).
_capture_ids () {
    _git for-each-ref --sort=v:refname --format='%(refname:short)' 'refs/tags/capture-c*' 2>/dev/null \
        | sed -n 's/^capture-\(c[0-9]*\)$/\1/p'
}
# The capture the store is restored to; empty when it is on the latest.
_current_capture () {
    _git symbolic-ref -q HEAD >/dev/null 2>&1 && return 0
    _git describe --tags --exact-match --match 'capture-c*' HEAD 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^capture-//'
}

# next / prev — step the restored store one capture forward or back, as
# 'restore' does: from the latest, 'prev' brings up the last capture (or the
# one before it, when the latest is that capture's own commit); 'next' past
# the last capture returns to the latest.
cmd_step () {
    local verb="$1"; shift
    case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) _cmd_help "$verb" ;; esac
    [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || _cmd_usage_die "$verb"
    _ensure_workspace
    local -a ids=(); local x cur pos=-1 n i
    while IFS= read -r x; do [[ -n "$x" ]] && ids+=("$x"); done < <(_capture_ids)
    n=${#ids[@]}
    (( n > 0 )) || die "no captures yet — nothing to step through."
    cur="$(_current_capture)"
    if [[ -z "$cur" ]]; then
        [[ "$verb" == next ]] && { info "already at the latest (step back with: provost prev)."; return 0; }
        pos=$n
        [[ "$(_git rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null)" == "$(_git rev-parse "capture-${ids[n-1]}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null)" ]] && pos=$((n - 1))
    else
        for i in "${!ids[@]}"; do [[ "${ids[i]}" == "$cur" ]] && pos=$i; done
        (( pos >= 0 )) || die "the store is at a commit that is not a capture — return with: provost restore latest"
    fi
    if [[ "$verb" == next ]]; then
        if (( pos + 1 >= n )); then cmd_restore latest; else cmd_restore "${ids[pos+1]}"; fi
    else
        (( pos > 0 )) || { info "already at the first capture (${ids[0]})."; return 0; }
        cmd_restore "${ids[pos-1]}"
    fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# files — a controlled directory's files as a capture kept them (files-<id>).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The newest capture whose files were kept ('' when none).
_latest_files_id () {
    _git for-each-ref --count=1 --sort=-v:refname --format='%(refname:short)' 'refs/tags/files-c*' 2>/dev/null \
        | sed -n 's/^files-\(c[0-9]*\)$/\1/p'
}

cmd_files () {
    local id="" path="" to="" a v
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        a="$1"
        case "$a" in
            -h|--help) _cmd_help files ;;
            --to) [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--to needs a directory."; to="$2"; shift 2; continue ;;
            --) shift; [[ $# -le 1 ]] || _cmd_usage_die files; path="${1:-}"; shift $#; continue ;;
            -?*) usage_die "unknown option for 'files': $a (see 'provost files --help')." ;;
        esac
        if [[ -z "$id" && -z "$path" ]] && v="$(_capid_of "$a")"; then id="$v"
        elif [[ -z "$path" ]]; then path="${a#./}"
        else _cmd_usage_die files; fi
        shift
    done
    [[ -d "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.git" ]] || die "no store yet — capture something first."
    if [[ -z "$id" ]]; then
        id="$(_latest_files_id)"
        [[ -n "$id" ]] || die "no run files yet — they are kept for captures made in a controlled directory (see 'provost control')."
    fi
    local ref="files-$id"
    if ! _git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/$ref" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        _git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/capture-$id" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
            || die "no such capture: $id (see 'provost log')."
        die "capture $id kept no files (it was not made in a controlled directory, or PROVOST_FILES_MAX_SIZE was 0)."
    fi
    if [[ -n "$to" ]]; then
        [[ -z "$path" ]] || usage_die "--to writes out every file; leave out the path."
        if [[ -e "$to" ]]; then
            [[ -d "$to" && -z "$(ls -A "$to" 2>/dev/null)" ]] || die "$to exists and is not an empty directory."
        else
            mkdir -p "$to" || die "cannot create $to"
        fi
        _git archive "$ref" | tar -x -C "$to" || die "could not write the files of $id to $to."
        ok "wrote the files of $id to $to."
        return 0
    fi
    local kind=tree
    if [[ -n "$path" ]]; then
        path="${path%/}"
        kind="$(_git cat-file -t "$ref:$path" 2>/dev/null)" || die "no file '$path' in the files of $id (see 'provost files $id')."
    fi
    if [[ "$kind" == blob ]]; then
        _git cat-file blob "$ref:$path"
    else
        info "files of $id${path:+ under $path} (size, path):"
        _git ls-tree -r -l --full-tree "$ref" ${path:+-- "$path"} \
            | awk -F'\t' '{ split($1, m, " "); printf "%10s  %s\n", m[4], $2 }'
    fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# search — git grep over the store: dataset rows (default), the captured
# outputs (--output), or a controlled directory's run files (--files); as the
# store stands, or as it stood after a capture (--at).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cmd_search () {
    local pat="" what=rows at="" v
    local -a gopt=() names=()
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            -h|--help) _cmd_help search ;;
            -i|--ignore-case) gopt+=(-i); shift ;;
            -F|--fixed-strings) gopt+=(-F); shift ;;
            -w|--word-regexp) gopt+=(-w); shift ;;
            --output) what=output; shift ;;
            --files) what=files; shift ;;
            --at) [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--at needs a capture id (e.g. c0007)."
                  at="$(_capid_of "$2")" || usage_die "invalid capture id '$2' (expected e.g. c0007 or 7)."
                  shift 2 ;;
            -e) [[ $# -ge 2 && -z "$pat" ]] || _cmd_usage_die search; pat="$2"; shift 2 ;;
            --) shift; if [[ -z "$pat" && $# -gt 0 ]]; then pat="$1"; shift; fi; names+=("$@"); break ;;
            -?*) usage_die "unknown option for 'search': $1 (see 'provost search --help')." ;;
            *) if [[ -z "$pat" ]]; then pat="$1"; else _require_name "$1"; names+=("$1"); fi; shift ;;
        esac
    done
    [[ -n "$pat" ]] || _cmd_usage_die search
    [[ -d "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.git" ]] || die "no store yet — capture something first."
    local rev=HEAD
    if [[ "$what" == files ]]; then
        (( ${#names[@]} == 0 )) || usage_die "--files searches every file a run kept; leave out dataset names."
        if [[ -z "$at" ]]; then at="$(_latest_files_id)"
            [[ -n "$at" ]] || die "no run files yet — they are kept for captures made in a controlled directory (see 'provost control')."
        fi
        _git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/files-$at" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "capture $at kept no files (see 'provost files')."
        info "files of $at matching '$pat' (path:line:text):"
        _git grep -I -n "${gopt[@]}" -e "$pat" "files-$at" -- 2>/dev/null | sed "s/^files-$at://"
        return 0
    fi
    if [[ -n "$at" ]]; then
        _git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/capture-$at" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "no such capture: $at (see 'provost log')."
        rev="capture-$at"
    fi
    _mktempd search; local hits="$_MKTEMPD/hits"
    if [[ "$what" == output ]]; then
        _git grep -I -n "${gopt[@]}" -e "$pat" "$rev" -- 'captures/*/output.txt' > "$hits" 2>/dev/null || true
        [[ -s "$hits" ]] || { info "no captured output matches '$pat'."; return 0; }
        # REV:captures/<id>/output.txt:LINE:TEXT -> capture, line, text
        R="$rev:" awk 'BEGIN { r = ENVIRON["R"]; print "capture\tline\ttext" }
            { s = substr($0, length(r) + 1); i = index(s, ":"); p = substr(s, 1, i - 1); s = substr(s, i + 1)
              j = index(s, ":"); split(p, P, "/"); print P[2] "\t" substr(s, 1, j - 1) "\t" substr(s, j + 1) }' "$hits" | _table
        return 0
    fi
    local -a spec=()
    if (( ${#names[@]} )); then for v in "${names[@]}"; do spec+=("datasets/$v.tsv"); done
    else spec=(datasets ':(exclude)datasets/unsorted.tsv'); fi
    _git grep -I -n "${gopt[@]}" -e "$pat" "$rev" -- "${spec[@]}" > "$hits" 2>/dev/null || true
    # REV:datasets/NAME.tsv:LINE:ROW — line 1 is the header, not a row.
    R="$rev:" awk -v OFS='\t' 'BEGIN { r = ENVIRON["R"] }
        { s = substr($0, length(r) + 1); i = index(s, ":"); p = substr(s, 1, i - 1); s = substr(s, i + 1)
          j = index(s, ":"); if (substr(s, 1, j - 1) == 1) next
          sub(/^datasets\//, "", p); sub(/\.tsv$/, "", p); print p, substr(s, j + 1) }' "$hits" > "$hits.rows"
    [[ -s "$hits.rows" ]] || { info "no dataset rows match '$pat'${at:+ at $at}."; return 0; }
    local ds prev="" first=1
    while IFS= read -r ds; do
        (( first )) || echo; first=0
        printf '%s (%s row%s)\n' "$ds" "$(awk -F'\t' -v d="$ds" '$1 == d { n++ } END { print n }' "$hits.rows")" \
            "$(awk -F'\t' -v d="$ds" '$1 == d { n++ } END { print (n == 1 ? "" : "s") }' "$hits.rows")"
        { _git show "$rev:datasets/$ds.tsv" 2>/dev/null | head -n 1
          awk -F'\t' -v d="$ds" '$1 == d { print substr($0, length(d) + 2) }' "$hits.rows"; } | _table
    done < <(cut -f1 "$hits.rows" | awk '!seen[$0]++')
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# diff — what changed between two captures: the rows each dataset got from
# them, and (in a controlled directory) the files.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cmd_diff () {
    local name="" a="" b="" v x
    local -a paths=()
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            -h|--help) _cmd_help diff ;;
            --) shift; paths=("$@"); break ;;
            -?*) usage_die "unknown option for 'diff': $1 (see 'provost diff --help')." ;;
        esac
        if v="$(_capid_of "$1")"; then
            if [[ -z "$a" ]]; then a="$v"; elif [[ -z "$b" ]]; then b="$v"; else _cmd_usage_die diff; fi
        elif [[ -z "$name" && -z "$a" ]]; then
            _require_name "$1"; name="$1"
        else _cmd_usage_die diff; fi
        shift
    done
    [[ -d "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.git" ]] || die "no store yet — capture something first."
    local main; main="$(_main_branch)"
    _mktempd diff; local tmp="$_MKTEMPD"
    # Default: the last two captures (that filed into NAME, when one is given);
    # one capture given: it against the one before it.
    local -a ids=()
    if [[ -n "$name" ]]; then
        _git show "$main:datasets/$name.tsv" > "$tmp/name.tsv" 2>/dev/null || die "no such dataset: $name (try 'provost ls')."
        _git show "$main:provenance.tsv" > "$tmp/name.prov" 2>/dev/null || : > "$tmp/name.prov"
        while IFS= read -r x; do [[ -n "$x" ]] && ids+=("$x"); done < <(awk -F'\t' -v P="$tmp/name.prov" '
            FILENAME == P { if (FNR > 1) pc[$1] = $3; next }
            FNR == 1 { col = ($1 == "#" && $2 ~ /^_*capture$/); next }
            { c = col ? $2 : pc[$1]; if (c != "" && !(c in seen)) { seen[c] = 1; print c } }' "$tmp/name.prov" "$tmp/name.tsv")
    else
        while IFS= read -r x; do [[ -n "$x" ]] && ids+=("$x"); done < <(_capture_ids)
    fi
    local n=${#ids[@]} i
    if [[ -z "$a" ]]; then
        (( n >= 2 )) || die "need two captures${name:+ of '$name'} to compare (found $n)."
        a="${ids[n-2]}"; b="${ids[n-1]}"
    elif [[ -z "$b" ]]; then
        b="$a"; a=""
        for i in "${!ids[@]}"; do [[ "${ids[i]}" == "$b" && $i -gt 0 ]] && a="${ids[i-1]}"; done
        [[ -n "$a" ]] || die "no capture${name:+ of '$name'} before $b to compare it with."
    fi
    for x in "$a" "$b"; do
        _git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/capture-$x" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "no such capture: $x (see 'provost log')."
    done
    # Read both captures' rows from the later capture's tree (it holds the
    # earlier one's rows too), so the store's own checkout does not matter.
    local late="$b"
    (( 10#${a#c} > 10#${b#c} )) && late="$a"
    mkdir -p "$tmp/t"
    _git archive "capture-$late" -- datasets provenance.tsv 2>/dev/null | tar -x -C "$tmp/t" 2>/dev/null || true
    local cmd_a cmd_b
    cmd_a="$(_git show "capture-$a:captures/$a/meta.tsv" 2>/dev/null | awk -F'\t' '$1 == "command" { print substr($0, 9) }')"
    cmd_b="$(_git show "capture-$b:captures/$b/meta.tsv" 2>/dev/null | awk -F'\t' '$1 == "command" { print substr($0, 9) }')"
    printf '%s%s → %s%s\n' "$a" "${cmd_a:+ ($cmd_a)}" "$b" "${cmd_b:+ ($cmd_b)}"
    local f ds any=0 prov="$tmp/t/provenance.tsv"
    [[ -f "$prov" ]] || prov=/dev/null
    for f in "$tmp"/t/datasets/*.tsv; do
        [[ -f "$f" ]] || continue
        ds="$(basename "$f" .tsv)"
        [[ -z "$name" || "$ds" == "$name" ]] || continue
        # Rows from A and from B, without the columns provost adds ('#',
        # capture, n): a row is the same when its data is. Removed rows in A's
        # order, added rows in B's, then a count of the rows both have.
        awk -F'\t' -v OFS='\t' -v P="$prov" -v A="$a" -v B="$b" -v DS="$ds" '
            FILENAME == P { if (FNR > 1) pc[$1] = $3; next }
            FNR == 1 {
                col = ($1 == "#" && $2 ~ /^_*capture$/); first = col ? 3 : 2
                if ($first == "n" || $first ~ /^_+n$/) first++
                hdr = ""; for (i = first; i <= NF; i++) hdr = hdr (i > first ? OFS : "") $i
                next
            }
            {
                c = col ? $2 : pc[$1]
                if (c != A && c != B) next
                r = ""; for (i = first; i <= NF; i++) r = r (i > first ? OFS : "") $i
                if (c == A) { ra[++na] = r; ca[r]++ } else { rb[++nb] = r; cb[r]++ }
            }
            END {
                if (!na && !nb) exit
                print ""
                if (!nb) { printf "%s: only in %s (%d row%s)\n", DS, A, na, na == 1 ? "" : "s"; exit }
                if (!na) { printf "%s: only in %s (%d row%s)\n", DS, B, nb, nb == 1 ? "" : "s"; exit }
                for (r in cb) left[r] = cb[r]
                out = ""; same = 0
                for (i = 1; i <= na; i++) { r = ra[i]; if (left[r] > 0) { left[r]--; same++ } else out = out "  - " r "\n" }
                for (r in ca) lefta[r] = ca[r]
                for (i = 1; i <= nb; i++) { r = rb[i]; if (lefta[r] > 0) lefta[r]-- ; else out = out "  + " r "\n" }
                if (out == "") { printf "%s: no change (%d row%s)\n", DS, na, na == 1 ? "" : "s"; exit }
                printf "%s:\n    %s\n%s", DS, hdr, out
                if (same) printf "  (%d row%s the same)\n", same, same == 1 ? "" : "s"
            }' "$prov" "$f" > "$tmp/out" || true
        if [[ -s "$tmp/out" ]]; then any=1; cat "$tmp/out"; fi
    done
    (( any )) || { echo; echo "no dataset rows from $a or $b${name:+ in '$name'}."; }
    # Files: only when both captures kept them.
    local fa=0 fb=0
    _git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/files-$a" >/dev/null 2>&1 && fa=1
    _git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/files-$b" >/dev/null 2>&1 && fb=1
    if (( fa && fb )); then
        echo
        if (( ${#paths[@]} )); then
            _git diff "files-$a" "files-$b" -- "${paths[@]}"
        else
            local st; st="$(_git diff --stat=100 "files-$a" "files-$b" 2>/dev/null)"
            if [[ -n "$st" ]]; then echo "files:"; printf '%s\n' "$st" | sed 's/^/  /'
                echo "  (the changes themselves: provost diff $a $b -- PATH)"
            else echo "files: no change"; fi
        fi
    elif (( ${#paths[@]} )); then
        die "$a and $b did not both keep the controlled directory's files."
    fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# listen / unlisten — capture a command every time you run it. Each listened
# command is a shell function in one file the user loads from their shell's
# startup file; the function runs it through 'provost run --show', so its
# output is on screen as always and a copy is filed (in the background at a
# terminal). Commands go to the store the directory routes to, as 'run' does.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_listen_file () { printf '%s/provost/listen.sh\n' "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}"; }
_listen_names () { local f; f="$(_listen_file)"; [[ -f "$f" ]] && sed -n 's/^\([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*\) () { command .* # provost listen$/\1/p' "$f"; return 0; }
_listen_write () {   # NAME... — rewrite the file with exactly these commands
    local f tmpf n prov; f="$(_listen_file)"
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$f")" || die "cannot create $(dirname "$f")"
    # How the functions call provost: by name when it is on PATH (so an
    # upgrade or move is picked up), else by this script's own path.
    prov="provost"
    command -v provost >/dev/null 2>&1 || prov="$(printf '%q' "$PROVOST_ROOT/provost")"
    tmpf="$f.tmp.$$"
    {
        printf '# provost listen: commands whose output provost files every time they run.\n'
        printf '# Load from your shell startup file:  . %s\n' "$(printf '%q' "$f")"
        printf "# Change it with 'provost listen CMD' / 'provost unlisten CMD'.\n"
        for n in "$@"; do
            printf '%s () { command %s run --show -- %s "$@"; } # provost listen\n' "$n" "$prov" "$n"
        done
    } > "$tmpf" && mv "$tmpf" "$f" || { rm -f "$tmpf"; die "cannot write $f"; }
}
_listen_hint () {
    local f q rc found=0; f="$(_listen_file)"; q="$(printf '%q' "$f")"
    for rc in "$HOME/.bashrc" "$HOME/.zshrc" "$HOME/.profile" "$HOME/.bash_profile"; do
        [[ -f "$rc" ]] && F="$f" awk 'index($0, ENVIRON["F"]) { f = 1 } END { exit !f }' "$rc" 2>/dev/null && found=1
    done
    if (( found )); then info "takes effect in new shells; in this one, run:  . $q"
    else info "load it in every shell by adding this line to ~/.bashrc (or ~/.zshrc):  . $q"
         info "and in this shell now:  . $q"; fi
}

cmd_listen () {
    case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) _cmd_help listen ;; esac
    local -a have=() n x
    while IFS= read -r x; do [[ -n "$x" ]] && have+=("$x"); done < <(_listen_names)
    if [[ $# -eq 0 ]]; then
        if (( ${#have[@]} == 0 )); then info "listening on nothing — 'provost listen CMD' to start."; return 0; fi
        printf '%s\n' "${have[@]}"
        return 0
    fi
    local -a add=()
    for n in "$@"; do
        [[ "$n" =~ ^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]{0,63}$ ]] || usage_die "'$n' is not a command name provost can listen on."
        [[ "$n" == provost ]] && usage_die "provost cannot listen on itself."
        case "$(type -t -- "$n" 2>/dev/null)" in
            builtin|keyword) usage_die "'$n' is a shell builtin; it has to run in your shell, so provost cannot run it for you." ;;
        esac
        command -v -- "$n" >/dev/null 2>&1 || warn "'$n' is not on PATH here; listening anyway."
        for x in ${have[@]+"${have[@]}"} ${add[@]+"${add[@]}"}; do [[ "$x" == "$n" ]] && continue 2; done
        add+=("$n")
    done
    if (( ${#add[@]} == 0 )); then info "already listening on: $*"; return 0; fi
    _listen_write ${have[@]+"${have[@]}"} "${add[@]}"
    ok "listening on: ${add[*]} — its output is shown as always and filed each time it runs."
    _listen_hint
}

cmd_unlisten () {
    case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) _cmd_help unlisten ;; esac
    [[ $# -ge 1 ]] || _cmd_usage_die unlisten
    local -a have=() keep=() gone=() x n
    while IFS= read -r x; do [[ -n "$x" ]] && have+=("$x"); done < <(_listen_names)
    for x in ${have[@]+"${have[@]}"}; do
        for n in "$@"; do [[ "$x" == "$n" ]] && { gone+=("$x"); continue 2; }; done
        keep+=("$x")
    done
    (( ${#gone[@]} )) || { info "not listening on: $*"; return 0; }
    _listen_write ${keep[@]+"${keep[@]}"}
    ok "stopped listening on: ${gone[*]}."
    info "in a shell that already loaded it, also run:  unset -f ${gone[*]}"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# control / uncontrol / instances — a directory as its own capture instance.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_registry () { printf '%s/instances.tsv\n' "$(_default_home)"; }

# Add DIR to the global registry once (dedup on the directory column),
# under the global store's lock.
_registry_add () {
    local dir="$1" reg home; reg="$(_registry)"; home="$(_default_home)"
    mkdir -p "$home"
    _lock "$home"
    [[ -f "$reg" ]] || printf 'directory\tcontrolled_at\n' > "$reg"
    if ! D="$dir" awk -F'\t' '$1 == ENVIRON["D"] { f = 1 } END { exit !f }' "$reg" 2>/dev/null; then
        printf '%s\t%s\n' "$dir" "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" >> "$reg"
    fi
    _unlock "$home"
}
_registry_remove () {
    local dir="$1" reg home; reg="$(_registry)"; home="$(_default_home)"
    [[ -f "$reg" ]] || return 0
    _lock "$home"
    local tmpf="$reg.tmp.$$.$RANDOM"
    if D="$dir" awk -F'\t' '$1 != ENVIRON["D"]' "$reg" > "$tmpf"; then
        mv "$tmpf" "$reg"
    else
        rm -f "$tmpf"
    fi
    _unlock "$home"
}

cmd_control () {
    case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) _cmd_help control ;; esac
    [[ $# -le 1 ]] || _cmd_usage_die control
    local dir="${1:-$PWD}"
    dir="$(cd "$dir" 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || die "no such directory: ${1:-$PWD}"
    local ws="$dir/.provost"
    if [[ -f "$ws/CONTROLLED" ]]; then _registry_add "$dir"; info "already controlled: $dir"; return 0; fi
    # Point this invocation's store at the new instance and initialise it.
    PROVOST_WORKSPACE="$ws"; PROVOST_STORE_KIND="controlled: $dir"; PROVOST_WORKSPACE_EXPLICIT=1
    _ensure_workspace
    _require_on_branch
    _lock
    printf '%s\n' "$dir" > "$ws/CONTROLLED"
    local n; n="$(_snapshot_context "$dir" "$ws")"
    _commit "provost: control $dir (context: $n file(s))"
    _unlock
    # Register in the global store so `provost instances` (run anywhere) sees it.
    _registry_add "$dir"
    ok "controlling '$dir' — captures run here now route to $ws (context: $n file(s))."
}

cmd_uncontrol () {
    case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) _cmd_help uncontrol ;; esac
    [[ $# -le 1 ]] || _cmd_usage_die uncontrol
    local dir="${1:-$PWD}"
    dir="$(cd "$dir" 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || die "no such directory: ${1:-$PWD}"
    [[ -f "$dir/.provost/CONTROLLED" ]] || die "not a controlled directory: $dir"
    local saved="$PROVOST_WORKSPACE"
    PROVOST_WORKSPACE="$dir/.provost"
    _lock
    rm -f "$dir/.provost/CONTROLLED"
    if _git symbolic-ref -q HEAD >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        _commit "provost: uncontrol $dir"
    fi
    _unlock
    PROVOST_WORKSPACE="$saved"
    _registry_remove "$dir"
    ok "released '$dir' (its .provost data is kept; captures here revert to the global store)."
}

cmd_instances () {
    case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) _cmd_help instances ;; esac
    [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || _cmd_usage_die instances
    local reg; reg="$(_registry)"
    [[ -f "$reg" ]] || { info "no controlled directories yet — 'provost control <dir>'."; return 0; }
    local n; n=$(( $(wc -l < "$reg") - 1 )); (( n < 0 )) && n=0
    (( n > 0 )) || { info "no controlled directories yet — 'provost control <dir>'."; return 0; }
    printf '%-40s %-20s %s\n' "DIRECTORY" "CONTROLLED_AT" "ACTIVE" >&2
    local d t
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r d t; do
        [[ "$d" == "directory" ]] && continue
        printf '%-40s %-20s %s\n' "$d" "$t" "$([[ -f "$d/.provost/CONTROLLED" ]] && echo yes || echo released)"
    done < "$reg"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# export / import — a portable bundle of the whole store (datasets + commits).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cmd_export () {
    local policy="" out="" gout
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            -h|--help) _cmd_help export ;;
            --redact) [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--redact needs a policy FILE (see 'provost export --help')."
                      policy="$2"; shift 2 ;;
            --redact=*) policy="${1#--redact=}"; shift ;;
            --) shift; [[ $# -le 1 ]] || _cmd_usage_die export; out="${1:-}"; shift $# ;;
            -?*) usage_die "export: unknown option: $1 (see 'provost export --help')." ;;
            *) [[ -z "$out" ]] || _cmd_usage_die export; out="$1"; shift ;;
        esac
    done
    _ensure_workspace
    if [[ -n "$policy" ]]; then
        [[ -n "$out" ]] || out="$PWD/provost-export-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)-redacted.bundle"
        _export_redacted "$policy" "$out"
        return 0
    fi
    if [[ -z "$out" ]]; then out="$PWD/provost-export-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ).bundle"; fi
    _git rev-parse HEAD >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "nothing to export — store has no commits yet."
    _lock
    gout="$(_git bundle create "$(_abspath "$out")" --all 2>&1)" || { printf 'git: %s\n' "$gout" >&2; die "export failed."; }
    _unlock
    [[ -f "$out" ]] || die "export failed."
    local sz; sz=$(stat -c '%s' "$out" 2>/dev/null || echo '?')
    ok "exported store → $out ($sz bytes). Import elsewhere with: provost import $(printf '%q' "$out") <dest>"
}

# --- redacted export (PV-033) ---------------------------------------------------
# 'provost export --redact POLICY' writes a copy of the store, history and
# all, in which every host name and address the policy picks out is replaced
# by a pseudonym made with a key that stays behind (host-1a2b3c4d5e6f,
# ip4-..., ip6-...), and every secret the capture-time scrub knows is
# scrubbed again (for captures filed with --no-redact or before 2.13.0). The
# same key gives the same pseudonyms, so two exports still join.
#
# _redact_policy FILE — read a policy: "KEY = VALUE" lines, '#' lines and
# blank lines skipped. Sets _RP_KEYFILE, _RP_IPV4, _RP_IPV6, _RP_DOMAINS,
# _RP_HOSTRES, _RP_COLS (newline-separated) and _RP_HOSTFILES (an array).
# A relative path is relative to the policy file's directory.
_RP_KEYS="key, ipv4, ipv6, domain, host, column, hosts"
_redact_policy () {
    local f="$1" dir line k v n=0
    [[ -f "$f" ]] || die "no such redaction policy: $f"
    dir="$(cd "$(dirname -- "$f")" && pwd)"
    _RP_KEYFILE=""; _RP_IPV4=1; _RP_IPV6=1; _RP_DOMAINS=""; _RP_HOSTRES=""; _RP_COLS=""; _RP_HOSTFILES=()
    while IFS= read -r line || [[ -n "$line" ]]; do
        n=$((n + 1)); line="${line%$'\r'}"
        [[ "$line" =~ ^[[:blank:]]*$ || "$line" =~ ^[[:blank:]]*# ]] && continue
        [[ "$line" == *=* ]] || die "$f, line $n: want KEY = VALUE ($_RP_KEYS), got: $line"
        k="${line%%=*}"; v="${line#*=}"
        k="${k//[[:blank:]]/}"
        v="${v#"${v%%[![:blank:]]*}"}"; v="${v%"${v##*[![:blank:]]}"}"
        [[ -n "$v" ]] || die "$f, line $n: '$k' has no value."
        case "$v" in "~/"*) v="$HOME/${v#\~/}" ;; esac
        case "$k" in
            key) case "$v" in /*) _RP_KEYFILE="$v" ;; *) _RP_KEYFILE="$dir/$v" ;; esac ;;
            ipv4|ipv6)
                case "$v" in
                    yes|on|1) v=1 ;; no|off|0) v=0 ;;
                    *) die "$f, line $n: $k is yes or no (got '$v')." ;;
                esac
                if [[ "$k" == ipv4 ]]; then _RP_IPV4="$v"; else _RP_IPV6="$v"; fi ;;
            domain)
                v="${v#\*.}"; v="${v#.}"
                [[ "$v" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9_]([A-Za-z0-9_.-]*[A-Za-z0-9_])?$ && "$v" != *..* ]] \
                    || die "$f, line $n: '$v' is not a domain (example.com, cluster.local)."
                _RP_DOMAINS+="$v"$'\n' ;;
            host)
                [[ "$v" != *$'\t'* ]] || die "$f, line $n: a host pattern cannot hold a tab."
                if ! awk -v re="$v" 'BEGIN { if ("x" ~ ("^(" re ")$")) {} }' </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1; then
                    die "$f, line $n: '$v' is not a regular expression awk can read."
                fi
                _RP_HOSTRES+="$v"$'\n' ;;
            column) _RP_COLS+="$v"$'\n' ;;
            hosts)
                case "$v" in /*) ;; *) v="$dir/$v" ;; esac
                [[ -f "$v" && -r "$v" ]] || die "$f, line $n: no such hosts file: $v"
                _RP_HOSTFILES+=("$v") ;;
            *) die "$f, line $n: unknown key '$k' (the keys: $_RP_KEYS)." ;;
        esac
    done < "$f"
    [[ -n "$_RP_KEYFILE" ]] || _RP_KEYFILE="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/provost/redact.key"
    return 0
}

# _redact_key FILE — the key (its first line) in _RP_KEY. A FILE that does
# not exist is made: 32 random bytes as hex, readable by its owner only. The
# key may not live in the store, or it would travel with every export.
_redact_key () {
    local f; f="$(_abspath "$1")"
    local ws; ws="$(cd -P -- "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE" 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || ws="$PROVOST_WORKSPACE"
    local fd; fd="$(cd -P -- "$(dirname -- "$f")" 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || fd="$(dirname -- "$f")"
    case "$fd/" in "$ws"/*) die "the redaction key $f is inside the store ($PROVOST_WORKSPACE), and would travel with it: keep it outside." ;; esac
    if [[ ! -e "$f" ]]; then
        mkdir -p -- "$(dirname -- "$f")" || die "cannot create $(dirname -- "$f") for the redaction key."
        ( umask 077; set -o noclobber; { od -An -tx1 -N32 /dev/urandom | tr -d ' \n'; echo; } > "$f" ) 2>/dev/null \
            || die "cannot write a new redaction key to $f."
        info "made a new redaction key: $f -- keep it: the same key gives the same pseudonyms, and it never goes into an export."
    fi
    [[ -r "$f" ]] || die "cannot read the redaction key $f."
    _RP_KEY=""; IFS= read -r _RP_KEY < "$f" || true
    _RP_KEY="${_RP_KEY%$'\r'}"
    (( ${#_RP_KEY} >= 32 )) || die "the redaction key in $f is too short: it needs 32 characters or more (move the file away and provost makes a random one)."
    [[ "$_RP_KEY" != *$'\t'* ]] || die "the redaction key in $f holds a tab."
}

# _sha256_many — for each file named on stdin (one a line; plain names),
# "NAME<TAB>sha256", from as few processes as xargs needs.
_sha256_many () {
    local -a tool=()
    if command -v sha256sum >/dev/null 2>&1; then tool=(sha256sum)
    elif command -v shasum >/dev/null 2>&1; then tool=(shasum -a 256)
    elif command -v openssl >/dev/null 2>&1; then tool=(openssl dgst -sha256 -r)
    else return 1; fi
    tr '\n' '\0' | xargs -0 "${tool[@]}" | awk '{ h = $1; f = substr($0, length(h) + 2); sub(/^[ *]/, "", f); print f "\t" tolower(h) }'
}

_export_redacted () {
    local policy="$1" out="$2" main t lib="$PROVOST_ROOT/lib" kid ncap
    [[ -f "$lib/provost_pseudo.awk" && -f "$lib/provost_redact.awk" ]] || die "provost's redaction code is missing from $lib."
    _redact_policy "$policy"
    _redact_key "$_RP_KEYFILE"
    out="$(_abspath "$out")"
    [[ ! -e "$out" ]] || die "$out already exists; name a new file."
    _git rev-parse -q --verify HEAD >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "nothing to export — store has no commits yet."
    _mktempd redact; t="$_MKTEMPD"
    main="$(_main_branch)"
    # The history to rewrite: the main branch and the capture tags. A
    # controlled directory's own files (files-* tags) stay behind.
    _git for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' 'refs/tags/capture-*' > "$t/refs"
    ncap="$(wc -l < "$t/refs")"; ncap="${ncap//[!0-9]/}"
    echo "refs/heads/$main" >> "$t/refs"
    _lock
    if ! _git fast-export --no-data --reencode=yes --signed-tags=strip --tag-of-filtered-object=drop \
            --stdin < "$t/refs" > "$t/stream" 2> "$t/err"; then
        _unlock; printf 'git: %s\n' "$(cat "$t/err")" >&2; die "export failed: git fast-export."
    fi
    _unlock
    # Every blob the history holds, and the paths it was filed under.
    LC_ALL=C awk '/^M / { p = substr($0, 3); i = index(p, " "); p = substr(p, i + 1); i = index(p, " ")
                         print substr(p, 1, i - 1) "\t" substr(p, i + 1) }' "$t/stream" | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$t/paths"
    cut -f1 "$t/paths" | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$t/blobs"
    LC_ALL=C awk -F'\t' '$2 ~ /^captures\/c[0-9]+\/meta[.]tsv$/ { print $1 }' "$t/paths" | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$t/metablobs"
    LC_ALL=C awk -F'\t' '$2 ~ /^datasets\/unsorted([.][^\/]*)?[.]tsv$/ { print $1 }' "$t/paths" | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$t/heldblobs"
    LC_ALL=C sort -u "$t/metablobs" "$t/heldblobs" | LC_ALL=C comm -23 "$t/blobs" - > "$t/otherblobs"
    : > "$t/hosts"
    local hf; for hf in ${_RP_HOSTFILES[@]+"${_RP_HOSTFILES[@]}"}; do cat -- "$hf" >> "$t/hosts"; echo >> "$t/hosts"; done
    export RP_IPV4="$_RP_IPV4" RP_IPV6="$_RP_IPV6" RP_DOMAINS="$_RP_DOMAINS" RP_HOSTRES="$_RP_HOSTRES" RP_COLS="$_RP_COLS"
    local -a aw=(env LC_ALL=C awk -f "$lib/provost_redact.awk" -f "$lib/provost_pseudo.awk")

    # 1. The names: everything the policy's rules pick out, anywhere.
    : > "$t/held"
    _git cat-file --batch < "$t/blobs" | "${aw[@]}" -v MODE=collect -v PATHS="$t/paths" \
        -v STREAM="$t/stream" -v HOSTS="$t/hosts" -v HELD="$t/held" > "$t/names.all" || die "export failed: collecting the names."
    LC_ALL=C sort -u "$t/names.all" > "$t/names"; rm -f "$t/names.all"
    # 2. A pseudonym for each: the first 12 hex digits of
    #    sha256(key LF kind LF name), after the kind.
    mkdir "$t/h"
    _RP_KEY="$_RP_KEY" LC_ALL=C awk -F'\t' -v D="$t/h" '{ f = D "/" NR; printf "%s\n%s\n%s", ENVIRON["_RP_KEY"], $1, $2 > f; close(f) }' "$t/names"
    printf '%s' "provost-redact-key-id"$'\n'"$_RP_KEY" > "$t/keyid"
    kid="$(_sha256 "$t/keyid")"; rm -f "$t/keyid"
    [[ -n "$kid" ]] || die "no sha256 tool (sha256sum, shasum or openssl) — cannot make pseudonyms."
    kid="${kid:0:12}"
    if [[ -s "$t/names" ]]; then
        ( cd "$t/h" && ls ) | ( cd "$t/h" && _sha256_many ) > "$t/hashes" || die "export failed: hashing the names."
    else
        : > "$t/hashes"
    fi
    rm -rf "$t/h"
    LC_ALL=C awk -F'\t' 'NR == FNR { H[$1] = $2; next }
        { p = (($1 == "host") ? "host-" : $1 "-") substr(H[FNR], 1, 12)
          if (H[FNR] == "") { print "provost: no hash for name " FNR > "/dev/stderr"; exit 3 }
          if ((p in SEEN) && SEEN[p] != $1 "\t" $2) { print "provost: two names share the pseudonym " p > "/dev/stderr"; exit 3 }
          SEEN[p] = $1 "\t" $2; print $1 "\t" $2 "\t" p }' "$t/hashes" "$t/names" > "$t/map" \
        || die "export failed: making the pseudonyms."
    # 3. Every blob but meta.tsv and 'unsorted', scrubbed and pseudonymised,
    #    one file each.
    mkdir "$t/new"
    _git cat-file --batch < "$t/otherblobs" | "${aw[@]}" -v MODE=blobs -v MAP="$t/map" -v OUTDIR="$t/new" \
        -v COUNTS="$t/c.blobs" -v USED="$t/u.blobs" || die "export failed: rewriting the files."
    # 4. Each capture's output.txt as exported, hashed for its meta.tsv,
    #    its commit's trailer, and 'provost verify'.
    LC_ALL=C awk -F'\t' '$2 ~ /^captures\/c[0-9]+\/output[.]txt$/ { split($2, P, "/"); print P[2] "\t" $1 }' "$t/paths" > "$t/outblobs"
    cut -f2 "$t/outblobs" | LC_ALL=C sort -u | ( cd "$t/new" && _sha256_many ) > "$t/out.sha" || true
    LC_ALL=C awk -F'\t' 'NR == FNR { H[$1] = $2; next } ($2 in H) { print $1 "\t" H[$2] }' "$t/out.sha" "$t/outblobs" > "$t/out256"
    # ...and, for a capture held in 'unsorted', in base64: that dataset's raw
    # column is the capture's input, which is its output.txt.
    local cid ob; : > "$t/b64"
    LC_ALL=C sort -u "$t/held" | while IFS= read -r cid; do
        ob="$(awk -F'\t' -v c="$cid" '$1 == c { print $2; exit }' "$t/outblobs")"
        [[ -n "$ob" && -f "$t/new/$ob" ]] || continue
        printf '%s\t' "$cid"
        if ! base64 -w0 < "$t/new/$ob" 2>/dev/null; then base64 < "$t/new/$ob" | tr -d '\n'; fi
        printf '\n'
    done > "$t/b64"
    # 5. meta.tsv: its values pseudonymised, input_sha256/input_bytes gone,
    #    output_sha256 the exported output's, and 'pseudonymised' added.
    _git cat-file --batch < "$t/metablobs" | RP_KEYID="$kid" "${aw[@]}" -v MODE=blobs -v META=1 -v MAP="$t/map" \
        -v OUTDIR="$t/new" -v OUT256F="$t/out256" -v COUNTS="$t/c.meta" -v USED="$t/u.meta" \
        || die "export failed: rewriting meta.tsv."
    # 5b. 'unsorted': each raw cell the base64 of its capture's exported
    #     output (of a row with no capture id, from before store format 3: a
    #     placeholder), everything else as in step 3.
    local gone; gone="$(printf '[REDACTED:held input, see its capture]\n' | { base64 -w0 2>/dev/null || base64 | tr -d '\n'; })"
    _git cat-file --batch < "$t/heldblobs" | "${aw[@]}" -v MODE=blobs -v UNSORTED=1 -v MAP="$t/map" \
        -v OUTDIR="$t/new" -v B64F="$t/b64" -v RAWGONE="$gone" -v COUNTS="$t/c.held" -v USED="$t/u.held" \
        || die "export failed: rewriting the 'unsorted' dataset."
    # 6. A new repository: the rewritten files, then the rewritten history.
    local -a ngit=(env -u GIT_DIR -u GIT_WORK_TREE -u GIT_INDEX_FILE -u GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY -u GIT_COMMON_DIR
                   git --git-dir="$t/new.git" -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null -c gc.auto=0)
    git init -q --bare "$t/new.git" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "export failed: cannot create a repository in $t."
    ( cd "$t/new" && LC_ALL=C sort "$t/blobs" | "${ngit[@]}" hash-object -w --stdin-paths ) > "$t/newshas" \
        || die "export failed: storing the rewritten files."
    LC_ALL=C sort "$t/blobs" | paste - "$t/newshas" > "$t/gitmap"
    "${aw[@]}" -v MODE=stream -v MAP="$t/map" -v GITMAP="$t/gitmap" -v OUT256F="$t/out256" \
        -v COUNTS="$t/c.msgs" -v USED="$t/u.msgs" < "$t/stream" > "$t/stream.new" \
        || die "export failed: rewriting the history."
    rm -f "$t/stream"
    if ! gout="$("${ngit[@]}" fast-import --quiet < "$t/stream.new" 2>&1)"; then
        printf 'git: %s\n' "$gout" >&2; die "export failed: git fast-import."
    fi
    "${ngit[@]}" symbolic-ref HEAD "refs/heads/$main" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    if ! gout="$("${ngit[@]}" fsck --no-progress 2>&1)"; then
        printf 'git: %s\n' "$gout" >&2; die "export failed: the rewritten history does not pass git fsck."
    fi
    if ! gout="$("${ngit[@]}" bundle create "$out" --all 2>&1)"; then
        rm -f -- "$out"; printf 'git: %s\n' "$gout" >&2; die "export failed: git bundle."
    fi
    # What was done.
    local nh n4 n6 nsec
    { cat "$t"/u.* 2>/dev/null || true; } | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$t/used"
    nh="$(awk -F'\t' '$1 == "host"' "$t/used" | wc -l)"; n4="$(awk -F'\t' '$1 == "ip4"' "$t/used" | wc -l)"
    n6="$(awk -F'\t' '$1 == "ip6"' "$t/used" | wc -l)"
    nsec="$({ cat "$t"/c.* 2>/dev/null || true; } | awk -F'\t' '{ n += $2 } END { print n + 0 }')"
    ok "exported a redacted copy of the store → $out: $((10#${ncap:-0})) capture(s); $((nh)) host name(s), $((n4)) IPv4 and $((n6)) IPv6 address(es) pseudonymised; $((nsec)) secret(s) scrubbed; key id $kid."
    info "      left out: each capture's input_sha256 (with it, a guessed name could be checked) and a controlled directory's own files (files-* tags)."
    info "      import with: provost import $(printf '%q' "$out") <dest>   (the key, $_RP_KEYFILE, stays here)"
}

cmd_import () {
    case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) _cmd_help import ;; esac
    [[ $# -ge 1 && $# -le 2 ]] || _cmd_usage_die import
    local file="$1" out
    [[ -f "$file" ]] || die "no such export file: $file"
    local dest="${2:-}"
    [[ -z "$dest" ]] && dest="$PWD/provost-import-$(basename "$file" .bundle)"
    dest="$(_abspath "$dest")"
    file="$(_abspath "$file")"
    # 'git bundle verify' needs a repository to check the bundle's
    # prerequisites against, and refuses to run outside one -- which made an
    # import from any ordinary directory fail as "not a valid bundle". An
    # export is --all and has no prerequisites, so verify it in an empty
    # scratch repository, not whatever one the caller happens to stand in.
    _mktempd import
    git init -q "$_MKTEMPD" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "cannot create a scratch repository in $PROVOST_TEMP_DIR (set TMPDIR)."
    git -C "$_MKTEMPD" bundle verify "$file" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "not a valid provost export bundle: $file"
    # mkdir is the atomic claim on the destination (two concurrent imports to
    # the same place cannot both win); the clone then fills the empty dir.
    [[ -e "$dest" ]] && die "destination already exists: $dest"
    mkdir "$dest" 2>/dev/null || die "destination already exists (or cannot be created): $dest"
    if ! out="$(git clone -q "$file" "$dest" 2>&1)"; then
        rm -rf "$dest"; printf 'git: %s\n' "$out" >&2; die "import (clone) failed."
    fi
    git -C "$dest" checkout -q main >/dev/null 2>&1 || git -C "$dest" checkout -q master >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    local nds ncap
    nds=$(find "$dest/datasets" -name '*.tsv' 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
    ncap=$(find "$dest/captures" -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -type d 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
    ok "imported → $dest ($nds dataset(s), $ncap capture(s))."
    info "browse it with: PROVOST_WORKSPACE=$(printf '%q' "$dest") provost ls   (or show/log/source/restore)"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# source — given a dataset row's #N index, show the original captured input.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cmd_source () {
    [[ $# -ge 1 ]] || _cmd_usage_die source
    case "$1" in -h|--help) _cmd_help source ;; esac
    [[ $# -eq 1 ]] || _cmd_usage_die source
    # A capture id (c0007, capture-c0007) shows that whole capture.
    if [[ "$1" =~ ^(capture-)?c[0-9]{1,12}$ ]]; then _source_capture "$(_capid_of "$1")"; return; fi
    local n="${1#\#}"                             # tolerate a leading '#'
    [[ "$n" =~ ^[0-9]{1,15}$ ]] || usage_die "row index must be a number (got '$1')."
    local prov; prov="$(_prov_file)"
    [[ -f "$prov" ]] || die "no provenance yet — capture something first."
    local ds="" cap=""
    IFS=$'\t' read -r ds cap < <(awk -F'\t' -v n="$n" 'NR > 1 && $1 == n { print $2 "\t" $3; exit }' "$prov") || true
    [[ -n "$cap" ]] || die "no row #$n (see 'provost ls' / 'provost show <dataset>')."
    _valid_capid "$cap" || die "provenance for row #$n names an invalid capture id — the store may be corrupt."
    local capd; capd="$(_cap_dir)/$cap"

    info "row #$n — dataset '$ds', from capture $cap"
    if [[ -f "$capd/meta.tsv" ]]; then
        echo; sed 's/\t/: /' "$capd/meta.tsv"
    fi
    if [[ -f "$capd/output.txt" ]]; then
        echo; printf '%s----- original source (capture %s) -----%s\n' "$C_DIM" "$cap" "$C_RST"
        cat "$capd/output.txt"
    else
        die "capture $cap has no stored output (workspace may be detached — try 'provost restore latest')."
    fi
}

# _source_capture ID — a capture's meta and original output: from the store's
# files when they are there, else from the capture's own commit (a capture
# after the one the store is restored to, or one whose files 'rm' purged).
_source_capture () {
    local id="$1" capd meta out
    capd="$(_cap_dir)/$id"
    if [[ -f "$capd/output.txt" ]]; then
        meta="$(cat "$capd/meta.tsv" 2>/dev/null || true)"; out="$capd/output.txt"
    else
        _git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/capture-$id" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
            || die "no such capture: $id (see 'provost log')."
        meta="$(_git show "capture-$id:captures/$id/meta.tsv" 2>/dev/null || true)"
        _mktempd source; out="$_MKTEMPD/output.txt"
        _git show "capture-$id:captures/$id/output.txt" > "$out" 2>/dev/null \
            || die "capture $id has no stored output."
    fi
    info "capture $id"
    if [[ -n "$meta" ]]; then echo; printf '%s\n' "$meta" | sed 's/\t/: /'; fi
    if [[ -f "$capd/annotations.txt" ]]; then
        echo; printf '%sannotations (added after the capture):%s\n' "$C_DIM" "$C_RST"; cat "$capd/annotations.txt"
    fi
    echo; printf '%s----- original source (capture %s) -----%s\n' "$C_DIM" "$id" "$C_RST"
    cat "$out"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# annotate / check-manifest — the run manifest (PV-020; see _prologue_kv).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# annotate ID KEY=VALUE... -- add keys decided after a run (reviewer,
# disposition, ...) to a filed capture, without filing it again: they go to
# captures/ID/annotations.txt as '#%' lines, one key each (setting a key again
# replaces its value), in a commit of their own. check-manifest reads them
# beside the capture's own prologue.
cmd_annotate () {
    case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) _cmd_help annotate ;; esac
    [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || _cmd_usage_die annotate
    local id kv k v
    id="$(_capid_of "$1")" || usage_die "not a capture id: '$1' (c0007, 7 or capture-c0007)."
    shift
    local -a keys=() lines=()
    for kv in "$@"; do
        k="${kv%%=*}"; v="${kv#*=}"
        [[ "$kv" == *=* && "$k" =~ $_MKEY_RE ]] \
            || usage_die "want KEY=VALUE (a key is letters, digits, '_', '.', '-'), got: '$kv'."
        [[ "$v" != *$'\n'* && "$v" != *$'\r'* ]] || usage_die "a value cannot hold a line break ($k)."
        keys+=("$k"); lines+=("#% $(_prologue_word "$k" "$v")")
    done
    _ensure_workspace
    _lock
    _require_on_branch
    local capd; capd="$(_cap_dir)/$id"
    [[ -f "$capd/meta.tsv" ]] || { _unlock; die "no such capture in the store: $id (see 'provost log')."; }
    local af="$capd/annotations.txt"
    {
        if [[ -f "$af" ]]; then
            KS="$(printf '%s\n' "${keys[@]}")" awk 'BEGIN { n = split(ENVIRON["KS"], K, "\n"); for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[K[i]] = 1 }
                { k = $0; sub(/^#%[ \t]*/, "", k); sub(/=.*/, "", k); if (k in S) next; print }' "$af"
        fi
        printf '%s\n' "${lines[@]}"
    } > "$af.tmp" && mv -- "$af.tmp" "$af"
    local list; list="$(printf '%s\n' "${keys[@]}" | awk '!s[$0]++' | paste -sd , - | sed 's/,/, /g')"
    _commit "annotate $id: $list"
    _unlock
    ok "$id: annotated ($list)."
}

# check-manifest [--profile P] [--post] [--strict] [ID|NAME...] -- each
# capture's run manifest against the profile: which required keys it lacks.
# No ID: every capture in the store; a NAME: the captures that fed dataset
# NAME. Prints capture, status (complete / incomplete) and the missing keys.
cmd_check_manifest () {
    local spec="" phase=run strict=0 a
    local -a args=()
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            -h|--help) _cmd_help check-manifest ;;
            --profile)
                [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--profile needs a profile NAME or file."
                spec="$2"; shift 2 ;;
            --profile=*) spec="${1#*=}"; shift ;;
            --post) phase=all; shift ;;
            --strict) strict=1; shift ;;
            --) shift; args+=("$@"); break ;;
            -?*) usage_die "unknown option for 'check-manifest': $1 (see 'provost check-manifest --help')." ;;
            *) args+=("$1"); shift ;;
        esac
    done
    local prof; prof="$(_manifest_profile "$spec")" || exit 1
    local capdir; capdir="$(_cap_dir)"
    [[ -d "$capdir" ]] || die "no captures yet -- capture something first."
    local -a ids=()
    local id
    if (( ${#args[@]} == 0 )); then
        while IFS= read -r id; do ids+=("$id"); done < <(
            for a in "$capdir"/c*; do [[ -d "$a" ]] && basename -- "$a"; done | sort -V)
    else
        local prov; prov="$(_prov_file)"
        for a in "${args[@]}"; do
            if id="$(_capid_of "$a")" && [[ "$a" =~ ^(capture-)?c[0-9]+$ ]]; then
                [[ -d "$capdir/$id" ]] || die "no such capture in the store: $id (see 'provost log')."
                ids+=("$id")
            else
                _require_name "$a"
                local before=${#ids[@]}
                if [[ -f "$prov" ]]; then
                    while IFS= read -r id; do
                        _valid_capid "$id" && [[ -d "$capdir/$id" ]] && ids+=("$id")
                    done < <(awk -F'\t' -v n="$a" 'NR > 1 && $2 == n && !s[$3]++ { print $3 }' "$prov")
                fi
                (( ${#ids[@]} > before )) || die "no captures fed a dataset '$a' (see 'provost ls')."
            fi
        done
    fi
    (( ${#ids[@]} > 0 )) || die "no captures yet -- capture something first."
    # (an id named twice, or by an ID and a NAME, is checked once)
    local -A seen=(); local -a uniq=()
    for id in "${ids[@]}"; do [[ -n "${seen[$id]+x}" ]] || { seen[$id]=1; uniq+=("$id"); }; done
    ids=("${uniq[@]}")
    local missing incomplete=0 tmp
    _mktempd checkman; tmp="$_MKTEMPD/table.tsv"
    {
        printf 'capture\tstatus\tmissing\n'
        for id in "${ids[@]}"; do
            missing="$(_manifest_missing "$prof" "$phase" "$capdir/$id/context.txt" "$capdir/$id/annotations.txt" | paste -sd , -)"
            if [[ -n "$missing" ]]; then
                incomplete=$((incomplete + 1)); printf '%s\tincomplete\t%s\n' "$id" "$missing"
            else
                printf '%s\tcomplete\t-\n' "$id"
            fi
        done
    } > "$tmp"
    _table < "$tmp"
    local what="the run-time keys"; [[ "$phase" == all ]] && what="every key, post-run ones too"
    info "${#ids[@]} capture(s) checked against profile $(basename -- "$prof" .tsv) ($what): $(( ${#ids[@]} - incomplete )) complete, $incomplete incomplete."
    if (( incomplete > 0 && strict )); then exit 1; fi
    return 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# stats — statistics on a dataset (the bundled analyze engine).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cmd_stats () {
    [[ $# -ge 1 ]] || _cmd_usage_die stats
    case "$1" in -h|--help) _cmd_help stats ;; esac
    local name="$1"; shift
    _require_name "$name"
    local f; f="$(_ds_dir)/$name.tsv"
    [[ -f "$f" ]] || die "no such dataset: $name (try 'provost ls')."
    local analyze; analyze="$(_find_analyze)" \
        || die "analyze engine not found (expected at $PROVOST_ROOT/analyze/provost-analyze)."
    # Datasets are TSV; suppress the gawk-preference banner. Extra args pass
    # straight through (e.g. --all, a numbered analysis list, --format json).
    PROVOST_ANALYZE_SUPPRESS_AWK_WARNING=1 _run_engine "$analyze" "$f" "$@"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ls / show / triage / doctor
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cmd_ls () {
    case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) _cmd_help ls ;; esac
    [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || _cmd_usage_die ls
    local dir; dir="$(_ds_dir)"
    [[ -d "$dir" ]] || { info "no datasets yet — pipe something into 'provost'."; return 0; }
    shopt -s nullglob
    local f name rows any=0
    printf '%-24s %8s\n' "DATASET" "ROWS" >&2
    for f in "$dir"/*.tsv; do
        any=1; name="$(basename "$f" .tsv)"
        rows=$(( $(wc -l < "$f") - 1 )); (( rows < 0 )) && rows=0
        printf '%-24s %8d\n' "$name" "$rows"
    done
    shopt -u nullglob
    [[ $any -eq 1 ]] || info "no datasets yet — pipe something into 'provost'."
}

# The 'unsorted' dataset holds each held-back capture as a single base64-encoded
# 'raw' column (see _ingest). Decode it back to text for display — in ONE awk
# (mawk-safe; LC_ALL=C so %c emits single bytes), not a base64 process per row —
# escaping embedded tabs/newlines so each captured input stays on one line.
# A value that is not valid base64 is shown as-is.
# 'provost source #N' remains the way back to the exact original bytes.
_decode_unsorted_tsv () {
    LC_ALL=C awk -F'\t' '
        BEGIN {
            b = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789+/"
            for (i = 1; i <= 64; i++) val[substr(b, i, 1)] = i - 1
            for (i = 1; i < 256; i++) ch[i] = sprintf("%c", i)
            ch[9] = "\\t"; ch[10] = "\\n"; ch[13] = "\\r"
        }
        # The raw column by name (a store made before format 3 has no
        # capture column); the capture id, when there is one, is shown too.
        NR == 1 { r = 2; for (i = 2; i <= NF; i++) { if ($i == "raw") r = i }
                  cc = (r > 2) ? 2 : 0
                  print "#\t" (cc ? $cc "\t" : "") "raw"; next }
        {
            s = $r; pre = $1 "\t" (cc ? $cc "\t" : "")
            if (s ~ /[^A-Za-z0-9+\/=]/) { print pre s; next }
            printf "%s", pre
            out = ""; acc = 0; bits = 0; n = length(s); cnt = 0
            for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
                c = substr(s, i, 1)
                if (!(c in val)) continue
                acc = acc * 64 + val[c]; bits += 6
                if (bits >= 8) {
                    bits -= 8; p = 2 ^ bits
                    byte = int(acc / p); acc -= byte * p
                    if (byte > 0) { out = out ch[byte]; cnt++ }
                    if (cnt >= 4096) { printf "%s", out; out = ""; cnt = 0 }
                }
            }
            printf "%s\n", out
        }' "$1"
}

_table () { if command -v column >/dev/null 2>&1; then column -t -s $'\t'; else cat; fi; }

cmd_show () {
    [[ $# -ge 1 ]] || _cmd_usage_die show
    local name="" at="" cap="" v out=_table
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            -h|--help) _cmd_help show ;;
            # --tsv: the rows as tab-separated text, exactly as stored (the
            # 'unsorted' raw column still decoded), never through column(1):
            # in an aligned table a blank cell is only spaces, and whether
            # the table has tabs or spaces depends on column(1) being
            # installed. What a program reads (provost_data.load, PV-031).
            --tsv) out=cat; shift ;;
            --at|--capture)
                [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "$1 needs a capture id (e.g. c0007)."
                v="$(_capid_of "$2")" || usage_die "invalid capture id '$2' (expected e.g. c0007 or 7)."
                if [[ "$1" == --at ]]; then at="$v"; else cap="$v"; fi
                shift 2 ;;
            -?*) usage_die "unknown option for 'show': $1 (see 'provost show --help')." ;;
            *) [[ -z "$name" ]] || _cmd_usage_die show; name="$1"; shift ;;
        esac
    done
    [[ -n "$name" ]] || _cmd_usage_die show
    _require_name "$name"
    local f prov
    if [[ -n "$at" ]]; then
        # The dataset as it stood just after capture AT, read from git: no
        # checkout, so the store stays where it is.
        _git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/capture-$at" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
            || die "no such capture: $at (see 'provost log')."
        _mktempd show; f="$_MKTEMPD/ds.tsv"; prov="$_MKTEMPD/prov.tsv"
        _git show "capture-$at:datasets/$name.tsv" > "$f" 2>/dev/null \
            || die "no dataset '$name' at capture $at."
        _git show "capture-$at:provenance.tsv" > "$prov" 2>/dev/null || : > "$prov"
    else
        f="$(_ds_dir)/$name.tsv"; prov="$(_prov_file)"
        [[ -f "$f" ]] || die "no such dataset: $name (try 'provost ls')."
    fi
    if [[ -n "$cap" ]]; then
        # Only the rows capture CAP filed: by the capture column, or, in a
        # store from before it (format 2), by provenance.
        _mktempd show; local only="$_MKTEMPD/only.tsv"
        [[ -f "$prov" ]] || prov=/dev/null
        awk -F'\t' -v P="$prov" -v C="$cap" '
            FILENAME == P { if (FNR > 1) pc[$1] = $3; next }
            FNR == 1 { col = ($1 == "#" && $2 ~ /^_*capture$/); print; next }
            (col ? $2 : pc[$1]) == C' "$prov" "$f" > "$only"
        f="$only"
        # None: a table says so; --tsv prints the header alone (no rows).
        if [[ "$(wc -l < "$f")" -le 1 && "$out" == _table ]]; then
            info "no rows in '$name' from capture $cap."; return 0
        fi
    fi
    if [[ "$name" == "unsorted" ]]; then _decode_unsorted_tsv "$f" | "$out"
    else "$out" < "$f"; fi
}

cmd_triage () {
    case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) _cmd_help triage ;; esac
    [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || _cmd_usage_die triage
    local f; f="$(_ds_dir)/unsorted.tsv"
    [[ -f "$f" ]] || { info "triage queue empty — nothing unmatched."; return 0; }
    info "unsorted rows (no-match / low-confidence / failed quality gate), raw input decoded ('provost source #N' for exact bytes):"
    _decode_unsorted_tsv "$f" | _table
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# rm — delete one or more datasets, provenance-aware, tracked as a commit.
#   provost rm [-f|--yes] [--no-purge] <dataset> [<dataset>...]
# Removes each dataset file (only the named dataset — variants like NAME.v2 are
# their own datasets, remove them by name), prunes the matching rows from
# provenance.tsv, and — BY DEFAULT — purges each captures/<id>/ dir that fed
# ONLY removed dataset(s) (a --multi capture that also fed a surviving dataset
# is kept, and its meta.tsv updated to drop the removed names). --no-purge /
# --keep-captures keeps every capture. The deletion is committed, so git's
# history (the commit before it) still holds the pre-deletion state; a purged
# capture's tags (capture-<id>, files-<id>) go with it, so 'restore' and
# 'files' no longer reach it. Row
# indices (#N) and capture ids are never reused.
#   provost rm [-f|--yes] --capture ID[,ID...]...
# Deletes captures instead: each captures/<id>/ dir and its tag, the rows it
# filed (by provenance; provost's capture column only for a row provenance
# does not know) from every dataset it fed, and their provenance rows. A
# dataset left with no rows is removed. One commit, as above, or nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cmd_rm () {
    local yes=0 purge=1 names=() caps=() parts=() v c
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            -f|--yes|--force)        yes=1; shift ;;
            --purge)                 purge=1; shift ;;
            --no-purge|--keep-captures) purge=0; shift ;;
            --capture|--capture=*)
                if [[ "$1" == --capture ]]; then
                    [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--capture needs a capture id (e.g. c0007)."
                    v="$2"; shift 2
                else
                    v="${1#*=}"; shift
                fi
                IFS=',' read -r -a parts <<< "$v" || true
                [[ ${#parts[@]} -gt 0 ]] || usage_die "--capture needs a capture id (e.g. c0007)."
                for v in "${parts[@]}"; do
                    c="$(_capid_of "$v")" || usage_die "invalid capture id '$v' (expected e.g. c0007 or 7)."
                    caps+=("$c")
                done ;;
            -h|--help)               _cmd_help rm ;;
            --)                      shift; while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do names+=("$1"); shift; done ;;
            -?*)                     usage_die "unknown option for 'rm': $1 (see 'provost rm --help')." ;;
            *)                       names+=("$1"); shift ;;
        esac
    done
    if [[ ${#caps[@]} -gt 0 ]]; then
        [[ ${#names[@]} -eq 0 ]] || usage_die "rm takes dataset names or --capture, not both."
        _ensure_workspace
        _require_on_branch
        _rm_captures "$yes" "${caps[@]}"
        return
    fi
    [[ ${#names[@]} -gt 0 ]] || _cmd_usage_die rm
    local n
    for n in "${names[@]}"; do _require_name "$n"; done
    _ensure_workspace
    _require_on_branch

    local dir; dir="$(_ds_dir)"
    # Validate every name up front — refuse the whole operation if any is missing.
    local missing=()
    for n in "${names[@]}"; do
        [[ -f "$dir/$n.tsv" ]] || missing+=("$n")
    done
    [[ ${#missing[@]} -eq 0 ]] || die "no such dataset: ${missing[*]} (try 'provost ls')."

    # Confirm (unless -f), reporting how many rows go with the datasets.
    local total=0 rows
    for n in "${names[@]}"; do
        rows=$(( $(wc -l < "$dir/$n.tsv") - 1 )); (( rows < 0 )) && rows=0
        total=$((total + rows))
    done
    if [[ $yes -ne 1 ]]; then
        printf 'About to delete %d dataset(s), %d row(s): %s\n' "${#names[@]}" "$total" "${names[*]}" >&2
        if [[ $purge -eq 1 ]]; then
            printf 'Also purging the captures that fed only them — originals removed (--no-purge to keep them).\n' >&2
        else
            printf 'Keeping the source captures (--no-purge).\n' >&2
        fi
        printf '%s' 'Proceed? [y/N] ' >&2
        local ans=""; read -r ans || ans=""
        case "$ans" in y|Y|yes|YES|Yes) ;; *) info "aborted — nothing deleted."; return 0 ;; esac
    fi

    _lock
    _require_on_branch

    # The removed names, newline-joined, drive the awk membership tests.
    local rmset; rmset="$(printf '%s\n' "${names[@]}")"

    # Drop the dataset files.
    for n in "${names[@]}"; do rm -f "$dir/$n.tsv"; done

    # Prune provenance rows whose dataset was removed (keep the header).
    local prov ptmp; prov="$(_prov_file)"
    if [[ -f "$prov" ]]; then
        ptmp="$(mktemp "$dir/.rmprov.XXXXXX")"
        if RMSET="$rmset" awk -F'\t' '
                BEGIN { n = split(ENVIRON["RMSET"], r, "\n"); for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) del[r[i]] = 1 }
                FNR == 1 { print; next }
                !($2 in del) { print }' "$prov" > "$ptmp"; then
            mv "$ptmp" "$prov"
        else
            rm -f "$ptmp"; die "could not prune provenance.tsv — nothing committed."
        fi
    fi

    # --purge: a capture goes only when NONE of the datasets it fed survive
    # (a --multi capture's meta 'dataset' is a comma-joined list). A capture
    # that still feeds a surviving dataset stays, with the removed names
    # dropped from its meta.tsv.
    local purged=0 updated=0 d cid cds part survivors any_removed
    local -a parts=()
    if [[ $purge -eq 1 ]]; then
        shopt -s nullglob
        for d in "$(_cap_dir)"/c*/; do
            [[ -f "$d/meta.tsv" ]] || continue
            cds="$(awk -F'\t' '$1 == "dataset" { print substr($0, 9); exit }' "$d/meta.tsv")"
            IFS=',' read -r -a parts <<< "$cds" || true
            survivors=0; any_removed=0
            for part in ${parts[@]+"${parts[@]}"}; do
                if grep -qxF -- "$part" <<< "$rmset"; then any_removed=1
                elif _valid_name "$part" && [[ -f "$dir/$part.tsv" ]]; then survivors=$((survivors + 1)); fi
            done
            [[ $any_removed -eq 1 ]] || continue
            cid="$(awk -F'\t' '$1 == "id" { print $2; exit }' "$d/meta.tsv")"
            if [[ $survivors -eq 0 ]]; then
                rm -rf "$d"
                if _valid_capid "$cid"; then
                    _git tag -d "capture-$cid" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
                    _git tag -d "files-$cid" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
                fi
                purged=$((purged + 1))
            else
                RMSET="$rmset" awk -F'\t' -v OFS='\t' '
                    BEGIN { n = split(ENVIRON["RMSET"], r, "\n"); for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) del[r[i]] = 1 }
                    { key[NR] = $1; val[NR] = substr($0, length($1) + 2); if ($1 == "dataset") dl = val[NR] }
                    END {
                        nd = split(dl, dn, ","); removed = ""; kept = 0
                        for (i = 1; i <= nd; i++) if (dn[i] in del) { drop[i] = 1; removed = removed (removed == "" ? "" : ",") dn[i] } else kept++
                        for (ln = 1; ln <= NR; ln++) {
                            k = key[ln]; v = val[ln]
                            if (k == "dataset" || k == "strategy" || k == "confidence" || k == "coverage" || k == "consistency") {
                                m = split(v, p, ","); v = ""
                                for (i = 1; i <= m; i++) if (!(i in drop)) v = v (v == "" ? "" : ",") p[i]
                            } else if (k == "dataset_rows") {
                                m = split(v, p, ","); v = ""
                                for (i = 1; i <= m; i++) { nm = p[i]; sub(/:.*/, "", nm); if (!(nm in del)) v = v (v == "" ? "" : ",") p[i] }
                            } else if (k == "datasets") v = kept
                            print k, v
                        }
                        print "removed_datasets", removed
                    }' "$d/meta.tsv" > "$d/.meta.tmp" \
                    || { rm -f "$d/.meta.tmp"; die "could not update $d/meta.tsv — nothing committed."; }
                mv "$d/.meta.tmp" "$d/meta.tsv"
                updated=$((updated + 1))
            fi
        done
        shopt -u nullglob
    fi

    local msg="rm: deleted ${#names[@]} dataset(s) [${names[*]}], pruned $total row(s)"
    [[ $purge -eq 1 ]] && msg="$msg, purged $purged capture(s)"
    (( updated > 0 )) && msg="$msg, updated $updated shared capture(s)"
    _commit "$msg"
    _unlock

    local extra=""
    (( updated > 0 )) && extra=" $updated capture(s) kept because they also feed a surviving dataset."
    if [[ $purge -eq 1 ]]; then
        ok "deleted ${names[*]} (${total} row(s)); purged $purged capture(s).$extra git's history (the commit before this one) still holds what was there."
    else
        ok "deleted ${names[*]} (${total} row(s)); source captures kept (--no-purge). 'provost source #N' still resolves surviving rows."
    fi
}

# _rm_cap_scan — for _rm_captures, on its variables: the rows the ids filed
# ($tmpd/rows), the datasets they fed (names), each dataset rewritten
# without them into $tmpd/ds.K (keep: K, or "" for one left empty), provenance without them ($tmpd/prov), and how many
# rows go (removed). A row is a capture's when provenance says so. provost's
# capture column (second, after '#'; `_capture` beside a data column named
# `capture`) is read only for a row provenance does not know, because a
# reconciled dataset can hold a data value in that position. A data column
# named `#` or `capture` is never matched. Returns 1 if a file cannot be
# read or written; the store is not touched.
_rm_cap_scan () {
    local n f k=0 pv=/dev/null before after
    names=(); keep=(); gone=(); removed=0
    rm -f "$tmpd"/ds.* "$tmpd/prov"; : > "$tmpd/rows"
    if [[ -f "$prov" ]]; then
        pv="$prov"
        awk -F'\t' 'FILENAME == ARGV[1] { del[$1] = 1; next }
            FNR > 1 && ($3 in del) { print $1 "\t" $2 }' "$tmpd/ids" "$prov" > "$tmpd/rows" || return 1
    fi
    # the datasets they fed, from provenance and from each meta.tsv
    while IFS= read -r n; do
        _valid_name "$n" && [[ -f "$dir/$n.tsv" ]] && names+=("$n")
    done < <( { cut -f2 "$tmpd/rows"
                ( cd "$cap" && sed 's|$|/meta.tsv|' "$tmpd/ids" | tr '\n' '\0' \
                    | xargs -0 awk -F'\t' 'FNR == 1 { seen = 0 }
                        !seen && $1 == "dataset" { n = split(substr($0, 9), d, ","); for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) print d[i]; seen = 1 }' )
              } | sort -u)
    for n in ${names[@]+"${names[@]}"}; do
        f="$dir/$n.tsv"; k=$((k + 1))
        awk -F'\t' -v ds="$n" '
            FILENAME == ARGV[1] { if ($2 == ds) drop[$1] = 1; next }
            FILENAME == ARGV[2] { del[$1] = 1; next }
            FILENAME == ARGV[3] { if (FNR > 1 && $2 == ds) known[$1] = 1; next }
            FNR == 1 { hc = ($1 == "#"); cc = (hc && $2 ~ /^_*capture$/); print; next }
            (hc && ($1 in drop)) || (cc && !($1 in known) && ($2 in del)) { next }
            { print }' "$tmpd/rows" "$tmpd/ids" "$pv" "$f" > "$tmpd/ds.$k" || return 1
        before=$(awk 'END { print NR }' "$f"); after=$(awk 'END { print NR }' "$tmpd/ds.$k")
        removed=$((removed + before - after))
        if (( after <= 1 )); then gone+=("$n"); keep+=(""); else keep+=("$k"); fi
    done
    if [[ -f "$prov" ]]; then
        awk -F'\t' 'FILENAME == ARGV[1] { del[$1] = 1; next }
            FNR == 1 || !($3 in del)' "$tmpd/ids" "$prov" > "$tmpd/prov" || return 1
    fi
    return 0
}

# _rm_cap_apply — for _rm_captures: the scan's files into the store, the
# capture dirs out of it, and the commit of those paths (rb) alone. Returns 1 at the first step that
# fails (never dies), so the caller can put the store back from HEAD.
_rm_cap_apply () {
    local n c k=0
    for n in ${names[@]+"${names[@]}"}; do
        if [[ -z "${keep[k]}" ]]; then rm -f "$dir/$n.tsv" || return 1
        else mv "$tmpd/ds.${keep[k]}" "$dir/$n.tsv" || return 1; fi
        k=$((k + 1))
    done
    if [[ -f "$tmpd/prov" ]]; then mv "$tmpd/prov" "$prov" || return 1; fi
    while IFS= read -r c; do rm -rf "${cap:?}/$c" || return 1; done < "$tmpd/ids"
    # only what it touches: anything else in the store is not this commit's
    _git add -A -- "${rb[@]}" >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
    if ! _git diff --cached --quiet -- "${rb[@]}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        _git commit -q -m "$msg" -- "${rb[@]}" >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
    fi
    return 0
}

# _rm_cap_commit — for _rm_captures, in a child in its own process group
# with INT/TERM/HUP ignored: apply and commit; on failure put the touched
# paths back from HEAD (tried again after a pause: git sets its own SIGINT
# handler, and another git may hold the index); after a commit, bring their
# index into line with HEAD and delete the tags. Exits 0 when committed
# ($tmpd/tagsfail, $tmpd/indexstale mark what did not follow), 1 when put
# back, 2 when it could not be put back.
_rm_cap_commit () {
    local tries put rmchild=""
    # The lock is this child's while it works: should the shell that took it
    # die first, the lock stays live (its owner, this pid, is running) and no
    # other provost writes beside it. _rm_cap_exit hands it back.
    # not ours any more (broken as stale and taken by another provost):
    # nothing has changed yet, so stop here
    _rm_cap_owner || exit 3
    if ! _rm_cap_apply && ! _rm_cap_landed; then
        tries=0; put=1
        while (( tries < 5 )); do
            _git checkout -q HEAD -- "${rb[@]}" >/dev/null 2>&1 && { put=0; break; }   # tree and index
            tries=$((tries + 1)); sleep 0.2
        done
        (( put == 0 )) && _rm_cap_exit 1
        _rm_cap_exit 2
    fi
    tries=0
    until _git reset -q -- "${rb[@]}" >/dev/null 2>&1; do
        tries=$((tries + 1)); (( tries < 5 )) || { : > "$tmpd/indexstale"; break; }; sleep 0.2
    done
    # their tags, capture-<id> and files-<id> where it kept files, in one go
    if _git for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' 'refs/tags/capture-c*' 'refs/tags/files-c*' > "$tmpd/allrefs" 2>/dev/null \
        && awk 'FILENAME == ARGV[1] { del["refs/tags/capture-" $1] = 1; del["refs/tags/files-" $1] = 1; next }
                ($1 in del) { print "delete " $1 }' "$tmpd/ids" "$tmpd/allrefs" > "$tmpd/refs"; then
        if [[ -s "$tmpd/refs" ]]; then
            _git update-ref --stdin < "$tmpd/refs" >/dev/null 2>&1 || : > "$tmpd/tagsfail"
        fi
    else
        : > "$tmpd/refs"; : > "$tmpd/tagsfail"
    fi
    _rm_cap_exit 0
}

# _rm_cap_landed — did this rm's commit land (HEAD moved on from the old HEAD
# to a commit of its own message), whatever git said?
_rm_cap_landed () {
    local h p m
    h="$(_git rev-parse -q --verify HEAD 2>/dev/null || true)"
    p="$(_git rev-parse -q --verify HEAD^ 2>/dev/null || true)"
    m="$(_git log -1 --format=%s 2>/dev/null || true)"
    [[ -n "$h" && "$h" != "$head0" && "$p" == "$head0" && "$m" == "$msg" ]]
}

# _rm_cap_owner — the child takes the store lock's owner record over, but
# only while it is still the record of the shell that took the lock
# ($rmowner): its own pid, and the time now (a long rm does not go stale by
# age). The record it wrote is $rmchild. Returns 1 if the record is no
# longer the shell's (the lock was broken and taken meanwhile).
_rm_cap_owner () {
    local host pid uid t now cur="" lock="$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.lock" rc=1
    now="$(date +%s)"
    # under _lock_break's own mutex: no break can fall between the compare
    # and the write (a breaker re-checks staleness holding it). Waited for a
    # little, as the hand-back does; one older than a minute was abandoned
    # (_lock_break's own rule) and is cleared.
    local w=0 cleared=0
    until mkdir "$lock.break" 2>/dev/null; do
        # cleared at most once: one that cannot be removed (not empty, not a
        # directory, another user's) is waited out like a busy one
        if (( ! cleared )) && (( $(_age "$lock.break") > 60 )); then
            cleared=1; rmdir "$lock.break" 2>/dev/null || true
        fi
        w=$((w + 1)); (( w < 30 )) || { : > "$tmpd/claimbusy"; return 1; }; sleep 0.1
    done
    { IFS= read -r cur < "$lock/owner"; } 2>/dev/null || true
    if [[ "$cur" == "$rmowner" ]]; then
        IFS=$'\t' read -r host pid uid t <<< "$rmowner"
        printf -v rmchild '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s' "$host" "$BASHPID" "$uid" "$now"
        printf '%s\n' "$rmchild" > "$lock/owner.tmp" 2>/dev/null \
            && mv "$lock/owner.tmp" "$lock/owner" 2>/dev/null && rc=0
    fi
    rmdir "$lock.break" 2>/dev/null || true
    return $rc
}

# _rm_cap_hand_back RECORD — write $rmowner back over the owner record, but
# only while it is still RECORD (never over another provost's lock).
_rm_cap_hand_back () {
    local cur="" lock="$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.lock" w=0
    [[ -n "$1" ]] || return 0
    # under _lock_break's mutex, as the claim; if it cannot be had the record
    # stays as it is (a dead owner's lock is broken as stale in time)
    until mkdir "$lock.break" 2>/dev/null; do
        w=$((w + 1)); (( w < 20 )) || return 0; sleep 0.1
    done
    { IFS= read -r cur < "$lock/owner"; } 2>/dev/null || true
    if [[ "$cur" == "$1" ]]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$rmowner" > "$lock/owner.tmp" 2>/dev/null && mv "$lock/owner.tmp" "$lock/owner" 2>/dev/null
    fi
    rmdir "$lock.break" 2>/dev/null || true
    return 0
}

# _rm_cap_exit CODE — hand the lock back to the shell that took it, and exit.
_rm_cap_exit () {
    _rm_cap_hand_back "$rmchild"
    exit "$1"
}

# _rm_captures YES ID... — rm --capture: the captures, and every row they
# filed. Ids go through files, not the environment or the command line, so
# a fleet's thousands of captures go in one commit. The store is git: what
# it touches must be committed before it starts, and any failure on the way
# (a signal included) puts those paths back from HEAD, so it either commits
# the whole deletion or leaves the store as it was.
_rm_captures () {
    local yes="$1"; shift
    local dir cap prov tmpd c count removed=0 msg shown _rmsig=0
    local names=() keep=() gone=() missing=() rb=()
    local GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1; export GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS
    dir="$(_ds_dir)"; cap="$(_cap_dir)"; prov="$(_prov_file)"
    mkdir -p "$dir"
    tmpd="$(mktemp -d "$dir/.rm.XXXXXX")" && tmpd="$(cd "$tmpd" && pwd)" \
        || die "cannot make a scratch directory in $dir."
    _TMPDIRS+=("$tmpd")          # removed on any exit, a signal's included
    printf '%s\n' "$@" | sort -u > "$tmpd/ids"
    while IFS= read -r c; do
        [[ -f "$cap/$c/meta.tsv" ]] || missing+=("$c")
    done < "$tmpd/ids"
    (( ${#missing[@]} == 0 )) || die "no such capture: ${missing[*]} (see 'provost log')."
    count=$(wc -l < "$tmpd/ids"); count=$((count + 0))

    if [[ $yes -ne 1 ]]; then
        _rm_cap_scan || die "could not read the store's datasets — nothing deleted."
        printf 'About to delete %d capture(s) and the %d row(s) they filed, from: %s\n' \
            "$count" "$removed" "${names[*]:-(no dataset)}" >&2
        printf '%s' 'Proceed? [y/N] ' >&2
        local ans=""; read -r ans || ans=""
        case "$ans" in y|Y|yes|YES|Yes) ;; *) info "aborted — nothing deleted."; return 0 ;; esac
    fi

    _lock
    _require_on_branch
    while IFS= read -r c; do
        [[ -f "$cap/$c/meta.tsv" ]] || { _unlock; die "capture $c went while waiting for the store — nothing deleted."; }
    done < "$tmpd/ids"
    # Under the lock, from what is there now.
    _rm_cap_scan || { _unlock; die "could not rewrite the datasets in $dir — nothing deleted."; }
    # What this touches, which HEAD must hold as it is so a failure can put
    # it back from there.
    if (( ${#names[@]} > 0 )) || [[ -n "$(_git ls-files -- "$dir" 2>/dev/null | head -n 1)" ]]; then
        rb+=("$dir")
    fi
    [[ -f "$prov" ]] && rb+=("$prov")
    rb+=("$cap")
    if [[ -n "$(_git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all -- "${rb[@]}" 2>/dev/null)" ]]; then
        _unlock
        die "the store has uncommitted changes to its datasets, provenance or captures (see: git -C $(printf '%q' "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE") status) — commit or discard them first; nothing deleted."
    fi

    local ilock; ilock="$(_git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null)/index.lock"
    if [[ -e "$ilock" ]]; then
        _unlock
        die "another git is using the store (its index.lock exists: a git status from a shell prompt or an editor, or one that crashed) — try again, or remove $ilock if nothing is running; nothing deleted."
    fi

    shown="$(head -n 8 "$tmpd/ids" | tr '\n' ' ')"; shown="${shown% }"
    (( count > 8 )) && shown="$shown …"
    # how to put it back by hand, worked out before anything changes
    local rbrel="" r
    for r in "${rb[@]}"; do rbrel+=" $(printf '%q' "${r#"$PROVOST_WORKSPACE"/}")"; done
    local wsq; wsq="$(printf '%q' "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE")"
    msg="rm: deleted $count capture(s) [$shown], pruned $removed row(s)"
    (( ${#gone[@]} > 0 )) && msg="$msg, emptied ${#gone[@]} dataset(s) [${gone[*]}]"

    local head0; head0="$(_git rev-parse -q --verify HEAD 2>/dev/null || true)"
    local rmowner="" i
    for i in ${_LOCKS_HELD[@]+"${!_LOCKS_HELD[@]}"}; do
        [[ "${_LOCKS_HELD[i]}" == "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.lock" ]] && rmowner="${_LOCKS_OWNER[i]}"
    done
    # From here to the last message signals wait (and set the exit status),
    # and errexit is off. Everything that changes the store runs in a child
    # in a process group of its own, so a Ctrl-C at the terminal (which goes
    # to the whole foreground group) reaches only this shell, which waits for
    # the child's outcome; a git it runs is never interrupted part way.
    trap '_rmsig=130' INT; trap '_rmsig=143' TERM; trap '_rmsig=129' HUP
    set +e
    rm -f "$tmpd/tagsfail" "$tmpd/indexstale"
    ( trap '' INT TERM HUP; set -m; _rm_cap_commit 2>"$tmpd/err" & echo "$!" > "$tmpd/cpid"; wait $! ) 2>/dev/null
    local outcome=$?
    if (( outcome == 3 )); then
        _unlock; set -e; trap 'exit 130' INT; trap 'exit 143' TERM; trap - HUP
        [[ -e "$tmpd/claimbusy" ]] \
            && die "another provost held the store's lock-break mutex ($PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.lock.break) throughout — nothing deleted; wait a minute and run it again (if it persists and nothing is breaking a lock, remove it)."
        die "the store's lock was no longer this rm's when it came to change the store (removed by hand, or broken as stale and taken) — nothing deleted; run it again."
    elif (( outcome > 2 )); then
        # The child died (a signal aimed at it alone). A git it started may
        # still be running in its group: wait for that (up to a minute), take
        # the lock's record back if the dead child's is still on it, then see
        # what the store says.
        local cpid="" w=0 cur="" host pid uid t
        { IFS= read -r cpid < "$tmpd/cpid"; } 2>/dev/null || true
        if [[ -n "$cpid" ]]; then
            while kill -0 -- "-$cpid" 2>/dev/null && (( w < 600 )); do sleep 0.1; w=$((w + 1)); done
        fi
        { IFS= read -r cur < "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.lock/owner"; } 2>/dev/null || true
        IFS=$'\t' read -r host pid uid t <<< "$cur"
        [[ -n "$cpid" && "$pid" == "$cpid" ]] && _rm_cap_hand_back "$cur"
        if _rm_cap_landed; then
            outcome=0
            # what it did not get to: tags still there, an index behind HEAD
            if _git for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' 'refs/tags/capture-c*' 'refs/tags/files-c*' > "$tmpd/allrefs" 2>/dev/null \
                && awk 'FILENAME == ARGV[1] { del["refs/tags/capture-" $1] = 1; del["refs/tags/files-" $1] = 1; next }
                        ($1 in del) { print "delete " $1 }' "$tmpd/ids" "$tmpd/allrefs" > "$tmpd/refs" 2>/dev/null; then
                [[ -s "$tmpd/refs" ]] && : > "$tmpd/tagsfail"
            else
                : > "$tmpd/refs"; : > "$tmpd/tagsfail"
            fi
            _git diff --cached --quiet -- "${rb[@]}" >/dev/null 2>&1 || : > "$tmpd/indexstale"
        else
            outcome=2
        fi
    fi
    _unlock
    set -e; trap 'exit 130' INT; trap 'exit 143' TERM; trap - HUP
    if (( outcome != 0 )) && [[ -s "$tmpd/err" ]]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$(tail -n 5 "$tmpd/err")" >&2
    fi
    case "$outcome" in
        0) ;;
        1)  (( _rmsig == 0 )) || { info "interrupted — the store is put back as it was, nothing deleted."; exit "$_rmsig"; }
            die "could not delete the captures — the store is put back as it was, nothing deleted." ;;
        *)  die "could not delete the captures, nor put the store back. Restore it with: git -C $wsq checkout HEAD --$rbrel" ;;
    esac

    local tagged="Their tags went with them; git"
    if [[ -e "$tmpd/tagsfail" ]]; then
        local nt more=""; nt=$( { wc -l < "$tmpd/refs"; } 2>/dev/null || echo 0); nt=$((nt + 0))
        if (( nt > 0 )); then
            (( nt > 8 )) && more=" ($((nt - 8)) more of them: git -C $wsq tag -l 'capture-c*' 'files-c*')"
            warn "deleted, but could not delete their tags: git -C $wsq tag -d $(sed 's|^delete refs/tags/||' "$tmpd/refs" | head -n 8 | tr '\n' ' ' | sed 's/ $//')$more"
        else
            warn "deleted, but could not list their tags to delete them: see git -C $wsq tag -l 'capture-c*' 'files-c*'"
        fi
        tagged="Their tags are still there (above); git"
    fi
    [[ -e "$tmpd/indexstale" ]] && warn "deleted, but the store's index is behind the commit: git -C $wsq reset -q --$rbrel"

    local extra=""
    (( ${#gone[@]} > 0 )) && extra=" ${gone[*]} had no rows left, and went too."
    ok "deleted $count capture(s) ($shown) and the $removed row(s) they filed.$extra $tagged's history (the commit before this one) still holds what was there."
    (( _rmsig == 0 )) || exit "$_rmsig"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# verify — check the store's integrity (PV-026).
#   provost verify [--capture ID[,ID...]]...
# 1. git fsck --full on the store.
# 2. No tracked file changed outside provost and left uncommitted.
# 3. Every capture-* tag names a commit on the main branch's history, and
#    every files-* tag a commit (those sit on their own chain, off main).
# 4. Each capture on the main branch has its tag, its output.txt there is the
#    one its tag filed, and that output re-hashes to the sha256 recorded in its
#    meta.tsv and in its commit's Output-SHA256 trailer. A capture filed before
#    hashes were recorded is counted as unhashed, not failed.
# 5. Capture ids run c0001..the highest with no gap that the store's history
#    cannot explain: an id 'rm' removed was committed once and is counted as
#    removed; an id that never appears in the history is a failure.
# Exit 0 when nothing failed, 1 with the findings. What this proves, and what
# it cannot (anyone who can rewrite the store's history can rebuild all of it):
# see 'provost help verify'.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cmd_verify () {
    local -a want=()
    local v
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            -h|--help) _cmd_help verify ;;
            --capture) [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || usage_die "--capture needs an ID."
                       IFS=',' read -r -a v <<< "$2"; want+=(${v[@]+"${v[@]}"}); shift 2 ;;
            --capture=*) IFS=',' read -r -a v <<< "${1#--capture=}"; want+=(${v[@]+"${v[@]}"}); shift ;;
            *) usage_die "verify: unknown argument: $1 (see 'provost help verify')." ;;
        esac
    done
    local -a norm=()
    for v in ${want[@]+"${want[@]}"}; do
        norm+=("$(_capid_of "$v")") || usage_die "verify: not a capture id: $v (ids look like c0007, 7 or capture-c0007)."
    done
    want=(${norm[@]+"${norm[@]}"})
    [[ -d "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.git" ]] || die "no git-backed store at $PROVOST_WORKSPACE — nothing to verify."
    _mktempd verify; local tmp="$_MKTEMPD"
    local main tip nfail=0 out
    main="$(_main_branch)"
    tip="$(_git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/heads/$main^{commit}" 2>/dev/null)" \
        || die "the store has no '$main' branch — nothing to verify."
    _vfail () { printf '%sFAIL%s %s\n' "$C_ERR" "$C_RST" "$*"; nfail=$((nfail + 1)); }

    # 1. the object store
    if out="$(_git fsck --full --no-progress --no-dangling 2>&1)"; then
        printf '%s ok%s git fsck --full: clean\n' "$C_OK" "$C_RST"
    else
        _vfail "git fsck --full reports damage:"
        printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk 'NR <= 20 { print "     " $0 }'
    fi

    # 2. tracked files changed and not committed
    out="$(_git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no 2>/dev/null | head -n 20)" || true
    if [[ -n "$out" ]]; then
        _vfail "tracked files were changed outside provost and not committed:"
        printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed 's/^/     /'
    fi

    # 3 + 4. tags, and each capture's output against its recorded hash.
    # The main branch's history, once, so ancestry is a lookup.
    _git rev-list "$tip" > "$tmp/history"
    # Every capture/files tag: name, object type, peeled type, commit.
    _git for-each-ref --format='%(refname:short)%09%(objecttype)%09%(*objecttype)%09%(objectname)%09%(*objectname)' \
        'refs/tags/capture-*' 'refs/tags/files-*' > "$tmp/tags"
    awk -F'\t' 'NR == FNR { h[$1] = 1; next }
        { c = ($2 == "commit") ? $4 : ($3 == "commit" ? $5 : "")
          if (c == "") print $1 "\tdoes not name a commit (it names a " ($3 != "" ? $3 : $2) ")"
          else if ($1 ~ /^capture-/ && !(c in h)) print $1 "\tnames commit " substr(c, 1, 12) ", which is not on the store'"'"'s history" }' \
        "$tmp/history" "$tmp/tags" > "$tmp/badtags"
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r v out; do _vfail "tag $v $out"; done < "$tmp/badtags"

    # Captures on the main branch now.
    _git ls-tree --name-only "$tip" captures/ 2>/dev/null | sed 's|^captures/||' \
        | grep -E '^c[0-9]{4,12}$' > "$tmp/live" || true
    local -a ids=()
    if [[ ${#want[@]} -gt 0 ]]; then
        for v in "${want[@]}"; do
            if grep -qx -- "$v" "$tmp/live"; then ids+=("$v")
            else _vfail "$v: not a capture on the store's '$main' branch"; fi
        done
    else
        mapfile -t ids < "$tmp/live"
    fi
    # Each capture's commit-message trailers, in one git call.
    _git for-each-ref --format='@@@ %(refname:short)%0a%(contents)' 'refs/tags/capture-*' \
        | awk '/^@@@ capture-/ { next }
               $1 == "Output-SHA256:" && $2 ~ /^c[0-9]+$/ { print $2 "\t" $3 }' > "$tmp/trailers"
    # Each capture's meta.tsv as its tag filed it, in one git call.
    local id
    for id in ${ids[@]+"${ids[@]}"}; do
        printf 'capture-%s:captures/%s/meta.tsv %s\n' "$id" "$id" "$id"
    done | _git cat-file --batch='@@@ %(rest) %(objecttype)' 2>/dev/null \
        | awk '/^@@@ c[0-9]+ / { id = $2; next }
               / missing$/ { next }
               $1 == "output_sha256" && id != "" { print id "\t" $2 }' > "$tmp/recorded"
    local hashed=0 matched=0 unhashed=0 rec trl tblob mblob got
    for id in ${ids[@]+"${ids[@]}"}; do
        if ! tblob="$(_git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/capture-$id^{commit}:captures/$id/output.txt" 2>/dev/null)"; then
            if _git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/capture-$id" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
                _vfail "$id: its tag capture-$id has no captures/$id/output.txt"
            else
                _vfail "$id: on the '$main' branch but has no capture-$id tag"
            fi
            continue
        fi
        mblob="$(_git rev-parse -q --verify "$tip:captures/$id/output.txt" 2>/dev/null)" || mblob=""
        if [[ -z "$mblob" ]]; then
            _vfail "$id: its output.txt is gone from the '$main' branch"
        elif [[ "$mblob" != "$tblob" ]]; then
            _vfail "$id: its output.txt was changed after it was filed (it is no longer what capture-$id holds)"
        fi
        rec="$(awk -F'\t' -v id="$id" '$1 == id { print $2; exit }' "$tmp/recorded")"
        if [[ -z "$rec" ]]; then unhashed=$((unhashed + 1)); continue; fi
        hashed=$((hashed + 1))
        _git cat-file blob "$tblob" > "$tmp/out" 2>/dev/null || { _vfail "$id: cannot read its output.txt"; continue; }
        got="$(_sha256 "$tmp/out")"
        if [[ -z "$got" ]]; then
            die "no sha256 tool (sha256sum, shasum or openssl) — cannot re-hash captures."
        elif [[ "$got" != "$rec" ]]; then
            _vfail "$id: output.txt hashes to ${got:0:16}…, but its meta.tsv records ${rec:0:16}…"
        else
            matched=$((matched + 1))
        fi
        trl="$(awk -F'\t' -v id="$id" '$1 == id { print $2; exit }' "$tmp/trailers")"
        if [[ -z "$trl" ]]; then
            _vfail "$id: its meta.tsv records a hash, but its commit carries no Output-SHA256 trailer"
        elif [[ "$trl" != "$rec" ]]; then
            _vfail "$id: its commit's Output-SHA256 trailer (${trl:0:16}…) is not the hash its meta.tsv records (${rec:0:16}…)"
        fi
    done

    # 5. the id sequence (the whole store; not narrowed by --capture)
    local removed=0 gaps=0 max
    _git log "$tip" --format= --name-only --diff-filter=A -- 'captures/*/meta.tsv' 2>/dev/null \
        | sed -n 's|^captures/\(c[0-9][0-9]*\)/meta.tsv$|\1|p' | sort -u > "$tmp/ever"
    max="$(cat "$tmp/ever" "$tmp/live" | sed 's/^c//' | awk '$1 + 0 > m { m = $1 + 0 } END { print m + 0 }')"
    read -r removed gaps < <(awk -v max="$max" '
        FILENAME == ARGV[1] { ever[$1] = 1; next }
        { live[$1] = 1 }
        END { for (i = 1; i <= max; i++) {
                  id = sprintf("c%04d", i)
                  if (id in live) continue
                  if (id in ever) r++; else { g++; print id > "/dev/stderr" } }
              printf "%d %d\n", r, g }' "$tmp/ever" "$tmp/live" 2>"$tmp/gaps")
    if (( gaps > 0 )); then
        _vfail "$gaps capture id(s) never appear in the store's history (deleted by rewriting it?): $(head -n 8 "$tmp/gaps" | tr '\n' ' ')$( (( gaps > 8 )) && echo '…')"
    fi

    local scope="${#ids[@]} capture(s)"
    [[ ${#want[@]} -gt 0 ]] || scope="all ${#ids[@]} capture(s)"
    printf '   checked %s: %d of %d hashed matching their record' "$scope" "$matched" "$hashed"
    (( unhashed > 0 )) && printf ', %d filed before hashes were recorded (no hash to check)' "$unhashed"
    printf '\n'
    (( removed > 0 )) && printf '   %d capture id(s) removed by rm (in the history, not on %s): not a failure\n' "$removed" "$main"
    printf '   %s at %s\n' "$main" "$tip"
    if (( nfail > 0 )); then
        printf '%sverify: %d finding(s)%s\n' "$C_ERR" "$nfail" "$C_RST"
        return 1
    fi
    printf '%s ok%s verify: no findings\n' "$C_OK" "$C_RST"
    return 0
}

cmd_doctor () {
    case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) _cmd_help doctor ;; esac
    local engine rc=0
    if engine="$(_find_engine)"; then
        printf '%s ok%s extract engine: %s\n' "$C_OK" "$C_RST" "$engine"
    else
        printf '%serror%s extract engine not found (expected at %s/extract/)\n' "$C_ERR" "$C_RST" "$PROVOST_ROOT"; rc=1
    fi
    local analyze awkv=""
    if analyze="$(_find_analyze)"; then
        printf '%s ok%s analyze engine: %s\n' "$C_OK" "$C_RST" "$analyze"
        if ! command -v gawk >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            awkv="$(awk --version 2>/dev/null || awk -W version 2>/dev/null || echo awk)"
            printf '   note: gawk not found; analyze uses %s (some analyses may degrade)\n' "${awkv%%$'\n'*}"
        fi
    else
        printf '   analyze engine not found (expected at %s/analyze/)\n' "$PROVOST_ROOT"
    fi
    # Engine/transform copies inside a store are never used (code comes only
    # from the install dir) — say so, so nobody expects an override to work.
    local sub
    for sub in extract analyze transform; do
        if [[ -e "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/$sub" || -e "$(_default_home)/$sub" ]]; then
            printf '   %snote%s a %s/ tree inside the store is IGNORED — code loads only from %s\n' "$C_ERR" "$C_RST" "$sub" "$PROVOST_ROOT"
        fi
    done
    printf '   store: %s (%s)' "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE" "${PROVOST_STORE_KIND:-global}"
    [[ -d "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.git" ]] && printf ' [git-backed]'; printf '\n'
    if [[ -d "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.git" ]]; then
        local head
        if head="$(_git symbolic-ref -q --short HEAD 2>/dev/null)"; then
            printf '   branch: %s\n' "$head"
        else
            printf '   %swarning%s store is on a restored capture (detached) — captures are refused; provost restore latest\n' "$C_ERR" "$C_RST"
        fi
    fi
    if [[ -f "$(_rowid_file)" ]]; then
        printf '   next row index: #%s\n' "$(cat "$(_rowid_file)")"
    fi
    if [[ -f "$(_capid_file)" ]]; then
        local nc; nc="$(cat "$(_capid_file)" 2>/dev/null || true)"
        if [[ "$nc" =~ ^[0-9]{1,12}$ ]]; then printf '   next capture id: c%04d\n' "$((10#$nc))"
        else printf '   %swarning%s capture counter is corrupt: %s\n' "$C_ERR" "$C_RST" "$nc"; fi
    fi
    if [[ -d "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.lock" ]]; then
        printf '   note: store lock held by: %s\n' "$(tr '\t' ' ' < "$PROVOST_WORKSPACE/.lock/owner" 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)"
    fi
    printf '   min-confidence: %s\n' "$PROVOST_MIN_CONFIDENCE"
    if [[ "$PROVOST_REDACT" == 1 ]]; then
        if [[ -f "$PROVOST_ROOT/lib/provost_redact.awk" ]]; then printf '   secret scrub: on (each input is scrubbed before it is stored; --no-redact or PROVOST_REDACT=0 turns it off)\n'
        else printf '%serror%s secret scrub: on, but %s/lib/provost_redact.awk is missing: captures will fail\n' "$C_ERR" "$C_RST" "$PROVOST_ROOT"; rc=1; fi
    else
        printf '   secret scrub: off (PROVOST_REDACT=0): inputs are stored as they come\n'
    fi
    if [[ -s "$(_faillog)" ]]; then
        printf '   %swarning%s failed background captures:\n' "$C_ERR" "$C_RST"
        _show_failures 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/     /'
    else
        printf '   failed background captures: none\n'
    fi
    return $rc
}

cmd_help () {
    cat <<EOF
provost $PROVOST_VERSION — turn command output into data

Give provost a command's output: it works out the format and files it as a
table you can list, show, search and compare. Every capture is a git commit,
so every run stays viewable.

CAPTURE
    provost run CMD [ARGS...]     run CMD and file its output (dataset CMD)
    CMD | provost                 file piped output
    provost FILE                  file output saved in FILE
    provost batch FILE...         file many outputs at once, one commit
    provost listen CMD            file CMD's output every time you run it
    provost control               give this directory its own store; each run
                                  here also keeps the directory's files

LOOK
    provost ls                    list datasets
    provost show NAME             show a dataset as a table
    provost search PATTERN        find matching rows in every dataset
    provost log                   list captures (c0001, c0002, ...)
    provost source ID             a capture's original output
    provost diff [NAME]           what changed between the last two captures
    provost files [ID]            a controlled directory's files at a capture
    provost stats NAME            statistics on a dataset
    provost triage                output provost could not file, and why
    provost check-manifest        which captures lack run-manifest keys
                                  (requirement, test, build, system)

GO BACK
    provost prev / provost next   step the store back / forward one capture
    provost restore latest        return to the present

EXAMPLE
    provost run df -h             capture 'df -h' into dataset 'df'
    provost show df               see it: '#' numbers the row in the store,
                                  'capture' names the run it came from, 'n'
                                  its place in that run

OPTIONS (for run, piped or file input)
    --name NAME                   file into dataset NAME
    --show                        show the output as well as filing it
    --sync                        wait until it is filed
    --llm                         have Claude write a parser for output
                                  provost reads badly (sends that output to
                                  the Anthropic API; needs an API key)

MORE
    provost COMMAND --help        one command's usage (provost show --help)
    provost help advanced         every command, option and setting, and how
                                  provost decides what to file
EOF
}

cmd_help_advanced () {
    cat <<EOF
provost $PROVOST_VERSION — advanced help

COMMANDS
  Capture
    provost run [OPTIONS] [--] CMD [ARGS...]
        Run CMD and file its stdout+stderr as dataset CMD (sudo, env, timeout
        and other wrappers are looked through). Exits with CMD's status.
    CMD | provost [OPTIONS]        provost [OPTIONS] FILE
        File piped input, or a file, as a dataset named after the parser that
        read it (csv, free, aligned-columns, ...).
    provost batch [OPTIONS] FILE... [--from LIST]
        File many inputs as one commit, a capture each, parsed in parallel
        (BATCH CAPTURE below).
    provost listen [CMD...]        provost unlisten CMD...
        Capture CMD every time it runs (a shell function per command, in
        \${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/provost/listen.sh; provost says how to
        load it). No CMD: list them.
  Look
    provost ls                     list datasets and their row counts
    provost show NAME [--capture ID] [--at ID]
        A dataset as a table; --capture: only the rows capture ID filed;
        --at: the dataset as it stood just after capture ID.
    provost search PATTERN [NAME...] [-i -F -w] [--at ID] [--output | --files]
        git grep over the rows (or the captured outputs, or a controlled
        directory's run files), shown per dataset under its header.
    provost source N | ID          the input behind row #N, or a capture's
    provost diff [NAME] [A [B]] [-- PATH...]
        Rows only A filed (-) and only B filed (+), per dataset, and the run
        files that changed (with PATH: their full diff). Default: the last
        two captures, or NAME's.
    provost stats NAME [ARGS]      statistics (ARGS go to the analyze engine;
                                   --all for every analysis)
    provost triage                 the 'unsorted' bucket: what was held, why
    provost check-manifest [--profile P] [--post] [--strict] [ID|NAME...]
        The captures whose run manifest lacks a required key (RUN MANIFEST
        below).
    provost annotate ID KEY=VALUE...
        Add keys decided after the run (reviewer, disposition) to capture ID,
        in a commit of its own.
  History
    provost log [--failed]         list captures (--failed: failed background)
    provost restore ID | latest    check out capture ID (read-only) / return to
                                   the latest
    provost prev                   step the store back one capture
    provost next                   step it forward one capture
  Directories and stores
    provost control [DIR]          give DIR (default: here) its own store
    provost uncontrol [DIR]        release it (its data is kept)
    provost instances              list controlled directories
    provost files [ID] [PATH] [--to DIR]
        A controlled directory's files as capture ID kept them: list them,
        print PATH, or write them all out into DIR.
    provost export [--redact POLICY] [FILE]
        The whole store as one portable git bundle; --redact: a copy with
        host names and addresses pseudonymised (SECRETS AND REDACTION).
    provost import FILE [DEST]     open an exported store elsewhere to browse
  Upkeep
    provost rm [-f] [--no-purge] NAME...
        Delete datasets, and the captures only they used (--no-purge: keep
        those). The deletion is one commit, and git's history (the commit
        before it) still holds what was there.
    provost rm [-f] --capture ID[,ID...]...
        Delete captures, and the rows they filed in every dataset they fed
        (a dataset left with no rows goes too). One commit, as above.
    provost doctor                 check the engines and the store
    provost verify [--capture ID]  check the store's integrity: git fsck, each
                                   capture's output against its recorded
                                   sha256, tags, and gaps in capture ids
    provost help [advanced]        the short help / this text
    provost COMMAND --help         one command's usage

  An ID is a capture id: c0007, 7 or capture-c0007.

CAPTURE OPTIONS (run, piped input, FILE)
    --name NAME             file into dataset NAME
    --show, --tee           show the output (or pass the input through) as well
    --sync, --wait          file in the foreground (the default when not at a
                            terminal)
    --async                 file in the background (the default at a terminal)
    -q, --silent            no progress output
    --multi                 file every dataset the input holds (MULTIPLE
                            DATASETS below)
    --llm, --no-llm         try a template Claude wrote for the tool first
                            (LLM TEMPLATES below; default off)
    --model, --no-model     try the learned layout model before holding a
                            capture (LAYOUT MODEL below; default off)
    --auto-multi, --no-auto-multi
                            try segmenting before holding a capture
                            (AUTO-MULTI below; default on)
    --min-confidence N      file only at confidence N or more (0-101; 101
                            holds every capture for triage)
    --min-coverage N        quality gate: line coverage (0-100; 0 = off)
    --min-consistency N     quality gate: type consistency (0-100; 0 = off)
    --plugin-dir DIR        an extra directory of extract strategies
    --context-columns, --no-context-columns
                            make a '#%' prologue's key=value words columns
                            (METADATA PROLOGUE below; default off)
    --units MODE            base: a NAME[UNIT] column in base units after
                            each column with a readable unit (UNITS below;
                            off, the default, adds none)
    --manifest FILE         prepend FILE's KEY=VALUE lines as the run
                            manifest and check it (RUN MANIFEST below)
    --manifest-profile P    check the run manifest against profile P
    --strict-manifest, --no-strict-manifest
                            refuse to file a capture whose manifest lacks a
                            required key (default: file it, with a warning)
    --env, --env=modules, --no-env
                            record where the capture was taken in its
                            prologue (ENVIRONMENT CAPTURE below; default off)
    --redact, --no-redact   scrub secrets before storing (SECRETS AND
                            REDACTION below; default on)

THE STORE
    Captures go to a GLOBAL git store, so you can capture from any directory:
    \$PROVOST_HOME, or ~/.provost. Point PROVOST_HOME at a shared,
    group-writable directory to collaborate; a lock serialises writers. Only
    data lives in the store: provost's engines always load from its own
    install directory ($PROVOST_ROOT).

    'provost control DIR' gives DIR its own store (DIR/.provost): captures
    made in DIR or below it go there. Which store a capture uses:
    PROVOST_WORKSPACE if set, else the nearest controlled directory above the
    current one, else the global store.

    Every capture is one commit, tagged capture-<id>. Capture ids and row
    numbers are never reused. Every dataset row carries:
      #        its number, unique in the store ('provost source N')
      capture  the capture it came from (c0007; 'provost source c0007')
      n        its record number within that capture
    In a controlled directory, each capture also lists the directory's files
    (context/manifest.tsv) and commits the files themselves, tagged
    files-<id>: the directory's .gitignore is honoured, and a file over
    PROVOST_FILES_MAX_SIZE is only listed. Each run's files commit has the
    previous run's as its parent, so git shows the directory's history
    across runs (git log --stat files-c0007, in DIR/.provost).

    'restore ID', 'prev' and 'next' check out an earlier capture: ls, show
    and source then show the store as it was, and new captures are refused
    until 'provost restore latest'. 'show --at', 'source ID', 'search --at',
    'files' and 'diff' read any capture straight from git without moving the
    store. 'export' bundles everything, tags included ('export --redact': a
    copy with host names and addresses pseudonymised); 'import' opens it.

DATASET NAMES
    'provost run CMD ...' files into a dataset named after CMD's basename
    (sudo, doas, env, nice, ionice, nohup, time, command, exec, stdbuf and
    timeout are skipped, with their options). Piped input is named after the
    parser that read it (csv, free, aligned-columns, ...). --name sets it.
    Names: letters, digits, '.', '_' and '-', starting with a letter or
    digit, no '..'. A capture of the same name with different columns
    RECONCILES into the dataset (columns unioned) when at least
    PROVOST_RECONCILE_OVERLAP% of the columns are shared, else it goes to
    NAME.v2, NAME.v3, ...

FOREGROUND AND BACKGROUND
    At a terminal a capture is filed in the BACKGROUND and the prompt returns
    at once; from a script or a pipeline (stderr not a terminal) it is filed
    in the FOREGROUND, so 'cmd | provost; provost show x' is race-free.
    --async / --sync (or PROVOST_ASYNC=1 / 0) force either. A failed
    background capture is logged in the store, and every later command warns
    until you look at it with 'provost log --failed' or 'provost doctor'.

HOW A CAPTURE IS FILED
    1. A leading run of '#%' lines is set aside as context (METADATA PROLOGUE).
    2. Every extract strategy scores the input; exact formats and
       tool-specific parsers outrank generic ones. The engine may swap its
       top generic parse for one that keeps more lines (LINE COVERAGE) or
       types its columns better (COLUMN-TYPE CONSISTENCY).
    3. No match, or below --min-confidence: held in 'unsorted', with the
       reason.
    4. A generic parse is checked by the QUALITY GATE. With --llm, a template
       for the tool is tried first (LLM TEMPLATES). One the gate would hold
       is then tried segmented (AUTO-MULTI), then by the layout model
       (--model); if nothing passes, it is held in 'unsorted', with the
       reason.
    The whole input is kept with every capture, filed or held
    ('provost source').

LINE COVERAGE
    A capture reports its coverage: the share of the input's non-blank lines
    that reached the dataset (shown after the confidence, recorded as
    'coverage' in meta.tsv; not measured above 128 KB). Confidence says how
    sure the engine is of the FORMAT; coverage says how much of the input the
    parse KEPT. When the parse ranked first keeps under 60% of the lines (one
    section of a multi-section report, say) the engine tries a lossless
    key/value parse of the whole report (one row per key, list item and
    section) and files that instead if it keeps materially more. A capture
    still filed under 60% says so.

COLUMN-TYPE CONSISTENCY
    A parse can keep every line and still put values under the wrong headers:
    a date split over three columns, a multi-word name spread over Name / Id /
    Version, a key: value file read as a two-column table. The engine types
    every cell (number, size, date/time, address, version, path, word, ...)
    and records the share of cells that match their column's type as
    'consistency' in meta.tsv (not measured above 128 KB; a word cut in half
    counts as a mismatch). When the generic parse ranked first is below 95%
    and another generic parse within 20 confidence points keeps as many
    lines, types its columns better (95% or more) and mixes fewer columns,
    the capture is filed with that parse instead.

QUALITY GATE
    A GENERIC parse that keeps under --min-coverage % of the input's lines
    (default 80) or whose columns are under --min-consistency % type-
    consistent (default 70) is held in 'unsorted', with the reason, instead
    of filed as a table: a likely-wrong table is worse than none. On the
    rated benchmark sets this held 18 of 48 wrong parses and no fully right
    one. Tool-specific parsers and strict grammars (json, yaml, xml, toml)
    are exempt; a measurement missing on a very large input never holds a
    capture. Set either to 0 to turn that check off.

AUTO-MULTI (segmenting a weak parse)
    When the gate would hold a parse, provost first asks the engine to
    segment the input, as --multi does, and files the parts instead when the
    split is clearly better:
      - it really is a split (2 or more datasets);
      - every part passes the gate on its own (confidence, coverage,
        consistency); a part read by a tool-specific parser must keep
        --min-coverage % of its own lines too;
      - together the parts keep at least --min-coverage % of the lines, and
        10 points more than the single parse;
      - no part is a table under positional A/B/C... columns only that
        shows a lost header or shredded rows: a column of box chrome,
        mostly empty cells, or a header-like line right above it (a
        headerless table is filed, under A/B/C...);
      - a part read by the parser the gate rejected has judged column types;
      - parts of 3+ lines or 3+ rows hold at least half of it (no
        shattering into fragments; a part's size is its lines or its rows,
        whichever is more, so one line holding many records is no fragment).
    Otherwise the capture is held as before, and why segmenting did not help
    is printed and recorded ('auto_multi declined: ...' in meta.tsv). A
    segmented capture says so in its ok line and records 'auto_multi' (why
    the single parse was held), 'single_strategy' / 'single_coverage' /
    'single_consistency', the parts' joint 'coverage', and each part's
    'dataset_coverage' / 'dataset_consistency'. Names follow --multi, except
    that with a NAME the part whose columns match the single parse's keeps
    dataset NAME, so repeated captures of one command keep their rows in one
    dataset whether a given capture segments or not. Only captures the gate
    would hold pay for the second engine run.

LAYOUT MODEL (--model, PROVOST_MODEL=1)
    A capture the gate would still hold after AUTO-MULTI gets one more try:
    the learned layout model (extract/model/, the explicit-only
    'layout-model' strategy). It labels each line's role and where its fields
    start, decides each table's columns once for all its rows, and cuts every
    value from the input. Its table is filed (strategy 'layout-model') only
    when it passes the gate, keeps at least as many lines as the parse the
    gate held, and the input is one table or one key/value record. Where the
    engine also read the lines, an arbiter keeps the engine's field split on
    the lines it trusts more. Before that, AUTO-MULTI asks the model to
    re-read any part of its split the gate rejects; and when the
    whole-capture table is declined, the model cuts the report into its own
    uniform sections (a key/value block, a table, a list), filed under
    AUTO-MULTI's rules. meta.tsv records 'layout_model' (filed or declined,
    and why). Needs python3 (no packages); declines inputs over 600 lines.

LLM TEMPLATES (--llm, PROVOST_LLM=1)
    Claude writes a parser for a tool once; provost reuses it offline.
    A template is either regular expressions per kind of line (table rows,
    key/value lines, decoration to skip; format 1), or, for records that span
    lines, a small Python parser (format 2) run in a box: a separate
    interpreter, a few pure modules, no files or network, CPU and memory
    limits.

    Where templates come from: the store's llm-templates/ (NAME.json for
    --name or the run command, else any template whose detect pattern
    recognises the input), then PROVOST_LLM_TEMPLATE_DIRS, then the
    templates shipped with provost. When none fits and PROVOST_LLM_API_KEY
    or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set, Claude (PROVOST_LLM_MODEL) writes one from
    this capture: THAT SENDS THE CAPTURE'S TEXT TO THE ANTHROPIC API (at most
    400 lines / 64 KB), once per tool. A new template is kept, and committed
    with the capture, only when it passes; from then on every capture of the
    tool is parsed offline, deterministically and at no cost.

    When it is used: FIRST, on a capture the gate would hold (before
    segmenting and the layout model), and on a GENERIC parse the gate passed,
    before that parse is trusted (the gate cannot see a title or prompt read
    as the header; a template written for the tool's layout can). Never in
    place of an exact-format or tool-specific parser, save one: a yaml parse
    may be bettered by a template already stored for the tool (plain
    'Label: text' output is valid YAML), but Claude is never asked to write
    one over it. Every block it yields
    is measured and filed under the gate's rules: one block as the capture's
    dataset, several as NAME.<block>, AUTO-MULTI's way. Against a generic
    parse the gate passed, it must keep that parse's share of the data lines
    (within 10 points; 20 against text-lines or line-records; lines it marks
    as decoration not counted).

    NOTES: lines a template sets aside (decoration, or lines it cannot read)
    that still carry a word or a number are filed beside its datasets as
    NAME.notes (line, kind, text): a title, a version banner, a summary count
    or an error between the rows is kept, never lost; only rules and box
    glyphs are dropped. A tab or newline inside a value becomes a space.

    SKIP AUDIT: a skipped line is not covered for free. One with no letter or
    digit (a rule) is decoration; one that carries a word or a number counts
    as unread until Claude has judged its kind decoration. When Claude writes
    a template that sets such lines aside, it is shown their count and one
    line of each kind, once, to read them or confirm them; the kinds it
    confirms are kept in the template ("decoration").

    REPAIR: a template keeps its first and latest captures (its
    "examples"). With a key, when a stored template meets a kind of line it
    has not met before (one it leaves unread, or a set-aside one not judged,
    or far more set-aside lines than before), provost sends the template,
    those captures and this one to Claude once, to extend it. The extension
    is kept, with this capture added to its examples, only if it still reads
    every line the template read of every capture it was written from (the
    store keeps them all) and passes the same rules; otherwise the template
    is used as it was. A capture whose lines the template has all met is
    never sent.

    LEDGER: every capture a template files is recorded in the store's
    llm-templates/NAME.seen.tsv (capture, how it was filed, line counts,
    input hash), committed with the capture.

    REVIEW (PROVOST_LLM_JUDGE=1, the default): after Claude writes or
    extends a template, one more call shows Claude the capture, the template
    and the tables it makes, and asks for PASS or up to five structural fixes:
    a parent the rows do not keep (a section, a tree's parent, a trunk above
    its members), a record split across tables, a header half applied, a
    line kind misread, data set aside. The fixes go back to the writer once;
    the revision is kept only if it reads the capture at least as well,
    still reads the earlier captures and passes the gate, else the template
    as it was before the review is used. A failed review costs nothing but
    the call. The verdict is stored in the template ("judge").

    meta.tsv records 'llm_template' (filed by which template, or declined and
    why) and 'llm_skip_audit'. PROVOST_LLM_LOG names a file that gets one
    JSON line per API reply.
    Needs python3 (no packages).

MULTIPLE DATASETS (--multi)
    Some reports hold more than one kind of data: a key/value block, a table,
    and a banner best left alone. The default keeps whichever part the engine
    ranks first; --multi splits the input, parses each part on its own terms
    (a kv block and a table can even use different delimiters), and files
    each as its own dataset under ONE capture. Datasets are named by their
    strategy (prefixed NAME. for 'run' or --name, e.g. iostat.aligned-columns),
    so two parts that parse the same way but differ in shape land in X and
    X.v2. Row numbers run on across them, and 'provost source N' still
    returns the whole input. meta.tsv records 'datasets' (how many) and
    'dataset_rows' (each one's range).

METADATA PROLOGUE
    A leading run of '#%' lines on piped input is capture context, not data:
    it is set aside before format detection (so it cannot corrupt a parse),
    stored with the capture (context.txt), and shown by 'provost source'.
    Wrappers can prepend context unconditionally.

    With --context-columns (PROVOST_CONTEXT_COLUMNS=1) every key=value word
    in it also becomes a column on each of the capture's rows, after '#' and
    'capture': '#% host=web03 rack=r2' puts host and rack on every row, so
    one host's answers stay labelled in a dataset many hosts feed. Keys are
    letters, digits, '_', '.' and '-', starting with a letter or '_'; the
    last value of a repeated key wins, and other words stay context only. A
    data column of the same name keeps it, and the context column becomes
    _NAME; meta.tsv records which columns came from the context
    ('context_columns', key=column,...). A capture held in 'unsorted' is
    kept as it came.

    A value holding spaces is double-quoted: '#% build.toolchain="gcc 13.2"'
    (\" and \\\\ inside the quotes stand for " and \\). An unterminated quote
    is an ordinary word.

RUN MANIFEST
    A run manifest is a prologue with agreed key names, so that a result can
    be traced to the requirement it tests, the build it measured and the run
    that produced it. A profile names the keys: extract/manifests/default.tsv
    (req, test, test.version, build.commit, build.sha256, build.toolchain,
    sched.job, run.id, sut, operator, deviation, reviewer, disposition), or
    proposal.tsv (the same keys under flat names: rvtm_id, code_commit,
    system_id, ...). Each key says whether it is known at run time ('run')
    or decided after it ('post'), and whether it is required.

    Nothing is checked unless asked. 'provost check-manifest' lists every
    capture (or ID..., or the captures that fed dataset NAME) with the
    required run-time keys it lacks or leaves empty; --post checks the
    post-run keys too, and --strict exits 1 if any capture is incomplete.
    At capture time, --manifest FILE prepends FILE's KEY=VALUE lines (a
    value is the rest of its line, spaces included) as the prologue, and
    --manifest FILE or --manifest-profile P checks it as it is filed: a
    missing key is a warning, or with --strict-manifest an error that files
    nothing (exit 1). Post-run keys are never demanded at capture time:
    'provost annotate ID reviewer=jo disposition=pass' adds them to the
    capture later (captures/ID/annotations.txt, its own commit).

    The profile is --profile / --manifest-profile P, else
    PROVOST_MANIFEST_PROFILE, else the store's own manifest-profile.tsv (a
    profile kept at the store's root, for a store with its own key names),
    else 'default'. A NAME is looked up in ~/.config/provost/manifests,
    then in provost's own extract/manifests; anything with a '/' or ending
    .tsv is a file.

    '#' is the store's: a data column of that name (zypper's repo number, a
    template's row index) is filed as '#_2' (the first '#_K' the dataset
    does not already use), so the two never share a name, and meta.tsv
    records it ('renamed_columns', DATASET:#=#_2,...).

ENVIRONMENT CAPTURE
    --env (PROVOST_ENV=1) adds where a capture was taken to its prologue, as
    env.* keys: env.host, env.kernel (uname -sr), env.arch, env.os (the OS
    release: /etc/os-release, or sw_vers on macOS), env.modules (the loaded
    environment modules, Lmod's \$LOADEDMODULES), env.user, and inside a
    Slurm job its id, node list, cluster and partition (env.slurm.job,
    .nodes, .cluster, .partition). A reading the run-manifest profile claims
    is written under the profile's key instead (its 'env' column): the
    Slurm job id is sched.job under the default profile, job_id under
    proposal. --env=modules (PROVOST_ENV=modules) adds the loaded kernel
    modules (lsmod) as env.kernel.modules. Nothing is recorded that is not
    set, and nothing at all without --env.

    Environment variables come only from an allow-list, PROVOST_ENV_VARS:
    names or glob patterns, space- or comma-separated (default: OMP_* MPI_*
    OMPI_MCA_* I_MPI_* MPICH_* UCX_* CUDA_* NCCL_*; set it empty for none),
    each as env.var.NAME. Never all of them: an environment carries tokens
    and passwords. A variable whose name or value looks like a secret
    (extract/secrets.tsv: tokens, keys, passwords, credentials in a URL) is
    not recorded even when allow-listed: provost warns, and lists its name
    in env.withheld. A tab or line break in a value becomes a space.

    The readings are of the host provost runs on, taken once per command:
    for a batch of files gathered from other hosts they describe the host
    that filed them. A key the input's own prologue or --manifest FILE sets
    is theirs, and --env leaves it out. As with --manifest, input_sha256 is
    of the input as received and output_sha256 of what is stored, the
    readings included.

BATCH CAPTURE (provost batch)
    'provost batch FILE...' files each FILE as a capture of its own, as
    'provost FILE' would, but parses them in parallel (--jobs, default the
    CPUs, at most 16) and files them all under one hold of the store lock as
    ONE commit, tagged capture-<id> for each. Capture ids follow the order
    the files were named; meta.tsv records each one's 'input' path and its
    'batch' (the id range). --name applies to the files after it; --from
    LIST reads files one a line (NAME<TAB>PATH sets the name per file).
    Every file is checked before anything is filed; an empty one is skipped,
    and one that fails to parse is reported while the rest are filed (exit
    1). --llm parses one file at a time, so a new template is written once.
    The files of one name are usually one command's output from many hosts:
    the first is detected as any capture is, and the rest are read with its
    strategy first (detection is most of the time a small input takes),
    each kept only if that gives the same table -- the same strategy, the
    same header, coverage no lower -- and detected from scratch otherwise.
    meta.tsv says 'detection  reused (...)' where it was. Off with
    PROVOST_BATCH_REUSE=0, and under --multi, --model and --llm.

SECRETS AND REDACTION
    Secrets never belong in a store. Before an input is stored, every known
    secret pattern in it becomes [REDACTED:kind]:
      private-key     a PEM or PGP private key block, BEGIN to END
      authorization   the value of an Authorization: header
      bearer          a token after 'Bearer' (16 characters or more)
      url-password    the password in scheme://user:password@host
      aws-access-key, github-token, slack-token, vault-token, jwt
                      tokens in their own formats
      password, secret, token, api-key
                      the value after a key whose name ends that way:
                      DB_PASSWORD=..., "token": "...", --api-key ...
    The command line 'run' records, and --manifest lines, are scrubbed too.
    A value that says it is hidden or empty (yes, none, ***, <hidden>,
    \$VAR) is left alone, and so is a plain word after a token, secret or
    key name, or after a --flag (help text). meta.tsv records what was
    scrubbed as 'redacted': kind=n,... (password=1,private-key=1), none, or
    off. --no-redact (PROVOST_REDACT=0) files the input as it comes.

    The input's hashes: input_sha256 stays the hash of the input as it was
    RECEIVED, secrets and all, so it still matches a hash taken where the
    output was made; output_sha256 is of output.txt as stored, scrubbed,
    and is what 'provost verify' re-checks. So input_sha256 can confirm a
    guess at a weak password in a scrubbed capture: keep the store inside
    the boundary, and take data out with 'export --redact'.

    Host names and addresses are data -- a fleet dataset is keyed on them
    -- so captures keep them. 'provost export --redact POLICY' takes them
    out: in the copy it writes, every name the policy picks out (its
    domains, host patterns, dataset columns and hosts files; IPv4 and IPv6
    addresses) is replaced by a pseudonym made with a keyed hash
    (host-1a2b3c4d5e6f, ip4-..., ip6-...), the same in every file, commit
    message and dataset name. The key stays behind; the same key gives the
    same pseudonyms, so datasets exported at different times still join.
    Secrets are scrubbed again on the way out. input_sha256 does not go
    (with it, a guessed name could be checked); output_sha256 and the
    commit trailers are those of the exported output, so 'provost verify'
    passes on the import, and each meta.tsv says 'pseudonymised' with the
    key's id. 'provost export --help' lists the policy's keys.

UNITS (--units base)
    By default a unit stays where the tool printed it: in a column's name
    (Bandwidth_(MB/s)), in each cell (482MiB/s), or in a column beside the
    number (transfer + transfer_unit). Nothing is converted unasked.
    With --units base (PROVOST_UNITS=base) each column whose unit can be
    read exactly gains a column right after it, NAME[UNIT], with the same
    values as plain numbers in a base unit: B, B/s, bit, bit/s or s. The
    original column is kept. 9.4 GB/s and 9400 MB/s both become 9400000000
    in a [B/s] column; 940 Mbits/sec is 940000000 in a [bit/s] one. Bits
    are never turned into bytes, nor bytes into bits.
    Units are read by the letter: B and Byte(s) are bytes, bit(s) bits, and
    a short b bits only in a rate (Mb/s, Gbps); k, K, M, G, T, P, E are
    powers of 1000, Ki, Mi, Gi, Ti, Pi, Ei powers of 1024; times are ns,
    us (or µs), ms and s. A bare multiplier
    (7.8Gi, 1.2G, as free -h and df -h print) names no quantity, so it is
    not read; nor is a unit glued to a word in a column name (rxkB/s). The
    one exception: iperf prints its powers of 1024 as KBytes, MBytes and
    GBytes (its own source says so), so those are read as KiB, MiB and GiB
    in a dataset its parser filed, or one named iperf or iperf3.
    A column is converted only when every cell holding a digit can be read,
    and the arithmetic is done on the decimal digits, never in floating
    point, so the result is exact. meta.tsv says 'units  base', and the
    capture's units.tsv lists each column added: dataset, column,
    unit_column, base, from (name, cells or column NAME_unit) and read (each
    unit with its exact factor: MB/s=10^6, MiB/s=2^20, ms=10^-3).

SETTINGS (environment)
    PROVOST_HOME              the global store (default: ~/.provost)
    PROVOST_WORKSPACE         use this store, whatever the directory
    PROVOST_ASYNC             1 = always background, 0 = always foreground,
                              unset/auto = background only at a terminal
    PROVOST_QUIET             1 = no progress / ok messages (as --silent)
    PROVOST_REDACT            0 = store inputs as they come, secrets and all
                              (as --no-redact; default 1: scrub them)
    PROVOST_MIN_CONFIDENCE    file only at this confidence or more (0-101,
                              default: 50; 101 holds every capture)
    PROVOST_MIN_COVERAGE      quality gate: min line coverage % for a generic
                              parse (default: 80; 0 = off)
    PROVOST_MIN_CONSISTENCY   quality gate: min column-type consistency % for a
                              generic parse (default: 70; 0 = off)
    PROVOST_RECONCILE_OVERLAP % of columns shared to reconcile a changed shape
                              into the same dataset, not NAME.vN (default: 60)
    PROVOST_MULTI             1 = file every dataset an input holds (--multi)
    PROVOST_CONTEXT_COLUMNS   1 = a '#%' prologue's key=value words become row
                              columns (as --context-columns)
    PROVOST_UNITS             base = add NAME[UNIT] columns in base units (as
                              --units base); off = none (the default)
    PROVOST_MANIFEST_PROFILE  the run-manifest profile, a NAME or a .tsv file
                              (as --manifest-profile; asks for a check)
    PROVOST_MANIFEST_STRICT   1 = refuse a capture lacking a required manifest
                              key (as --strict-manifest; asks for a check)
    PROVOST_ENV               1 = record where each capture was taken (as
                              --env); modules = and the kernel modules
    PROVOST_ENV_VARS          the variables --env records: names or globs
                              (default: OMP_* MPI_* OMPI_MCA_* I_MPI_* MPICH_*
                              UCX_* CUDA_* NCCL_*; empty = none)
    PROVOST_BATCH_REUSE       0 = batch detects every input from scratch
                              (default 1: the rest of a name's inputs try its
                              first one's strategy)
    PROVOST_AUTO_MULTI        1 = segment a parse the gate would hold (default;
                              0 = off, as --no-auto-multi)
    PROVOST_MODEL             1 = try the learned layout model (as --model;
                              needs python3)
    PROVOST_LAYOUT_LM         with --model, label with the tiny pretrained LM
                              in this directory, not the trees; unset, the
                              installed provost-model package (pip install
                              provost[lm]); 0 = the trees only
    PROVOST_PYTHON            the python3 that runs the layout model (the
                              pip launcher sets it to its own)
    PROVOST_LLM               1 = use / have Claude write a template (as --llm)
    PROVOST_LLM_MODEL         the model that writes templates (default:
                              claude-opus-5-5)
    PROVOST_LLM_JUDGE         1 = review a template Claude writes or extends,
                              and revise it once (the default); 0 = don't
    PROVOST_LLM_API_KEY       Anthropic API key for --llm (else
                              ANTHROPIC_API_KEY; use this one where that is
                              taken, e.g. inside Claude Code)
    PROVOST_LLM_BASE_URL      API base URL (default: https://api.anthropic.com)
    PROVOST_LLM_TEMPLATE_DIRS colon-separated template directories, looked in
                              after the store's and before provost's own
    PROVOST_LLM_LOG           a file to append each API reply's usage to (one
                              JSON line: purpose, tool, model, tokens)
    PROVOST_PLUGIN_DIR        an extra strategy directory (as --plugin-dir)
    PROVOST_MAX_INPUT         max input bytes, piped or run (default: 10485760)
    PROVOST_FILES_MAX_SIZE    in a controlled directory, the largest file kept
                              with each run (bytes; default: 1048576; 0 = none)
    PROVOST_LOCK_TIMEOUT      seconds to wait for the store lock (default: 30)
    PROVOST_LOCK_MAX_AGE      seconds after which a lock is presumed stale even
                              if its owner cannot be checked (default: 3600)
    TMPDIR                    temp and background spool directories (default:
                              /tmp)
    XDG_CONFIG_HOME           where 'listen' keeps its file (default ~/.config)
    Numeric settings must be whole numbers; anything else is refused (exit 2).

See docs/0001-rename-and-simplification.md for the design.
EOF
}

# "did you mean" for a mistyped subcommand (edit distance ≤ 2, or a prefix).
_suggest_verb () {
    W="$1" awk 'function lev(a, b,    la, lb, i, j, d, c, x, y, z) {
            la = length(a); lb = length(b)
            for (i = 0; i <= la; i++) d[i, 0] = i
            for (j = 0; j <= lb; j++) d[0, j] = j
            for (i = 1; i <= la; i++) for (j = 1; j <= lb; j++) {
                c = (substr(a, i, 1) == substr(b, j, 1)) ? 0 : 1
                x = d[i-1, j] + 1; y = d[i, j-1] + 1; z = d[i-1, j-1] + c
                d[i, j] = (x < y ? (x < z ? x : z) : (y < z ? y : z))
            }
            return d[la, lb]
        }
        BEGIN {
            w = ENVIRON["W"]; n = split("run inject batch listen unlisten source control uncontrol instances export import log restore next prev files diff search ls show rm stats triage annotate check-manifest doctor verify help", v, " ")
            best = ""; bd = 3
            for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
                dist = lev(w, v[i])
                if (length(w) >= 2 && index(v[i], w) == 1 && dist > 1) dist = 1
                if (dist < bd) { bd = dist; best = v[i] }
            }
            if (best != "" && bd < length(w)) print best
        }'
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dispatch — bare invocation (or a leading option / FILE) means inject.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
main () {
    local verb="${1:-}"
    case "$verb" in
        help|-h|--help)
            case "${2:-}" in advanced|all|--all|-a) cmd_help_advanced; exit 0 ;; esac
            if [[ $# -ge 2 ]] && _usage_text "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1; then _cmd_help "$2"; fi
            cmd_help; exit 0 ;;
        -V|--version)
            printf 'provost %s\nCopyright (C) 2026 Martin J. Gallagher\nLicense: GPL-3.0-or-later <https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.html>\nThis is free software: you are free to change and redistribute it.\nThere is no warranty, to the extent permitted by law.\n' "$PROVOST_VERSION"
            exit 0 ;;
    esac
    _validate_settings
    _vet_store_config
    # Bare 'provost' on a terminal: there is nothing piped in — show help
    # instead of silently waiting on stdin.
    if [[ $# -eq 0 && -t 0 ]]; then cmd_help; exit 0; fi
    case "$verb" in
        doctor|log|verify) ;;                     # these show the failures themselves / read only
        *) _warn_failures ;;
    esac
    case "$verb" in
        run)           shift; cmd_run "$@" ;;
        batch)         shift; cmd_batch "$@" ;;
        source|src)    shift; cmd_source "$@" ;;
        control)       shift; cmd_control "$@" ;;
        uncontrol)     shift; cmd_uncontrol "$@" ;;
        instances)     shift; cmd_instances "$@" ;;
        export)        shift; cmd_export "$@" ;;
        import)        shift; cmd_import "$@" ;;
        log)           shift; cmd_log "$@" ;;
        restore)       shift; cmd_restore "$@" ;;
        next|prev)     cmd_step "$@" ;;
        files)         shift; cmd_files "$@" ;;
        diff)          shift; cmd_diff "$@" ;;
        search|find)   shift; cmd_search "$@" ;;
        listen)        shift; cmd_listen "$@" ;;
        unlisten)      shift; cmd_unlisten "$@" ;;
        ls)            shift; cmd_ls "$@" ;;
        show)          shift; cmd_show "$@" ;;
        rm|delete|drop) shift; cmd_rm "$@" ;;
        stats)         shift; cmd_stats "$@" ;;
        triage)        shift; cmd_triage "$@" ;;
        annotate)      shift; cmd_annotate "$@" ;;
        check-manifest) shift; cmd_check_manifest "$@" ;;
        doctor)        shift; cmd_doctor "$@" ;;
        verify)        shift; cmd_verify "$@" ;;
        inject)        shift; cmd_inject "$@" ;;   # explicit alias
        ""|-*)         cmd_inject "$@" ;;          # default verb (options only)
        *)
            # A lone word: a FILE to inject if it exists (or looks like a path),
            # otherwise a mistyped subcommand.
            if [[ -e "$verb" || "$verb" == */* ]]; then cmd_inject "$@"; return; fi
            local s; s="$(_suggest_verb "$verb")"
            if [[ -n "$s" ]]; then
                usage_die "unknown command '$verb' (did you mean '$s'?)"
            fi
            usage_die "unknown command '$verb' (and no such file) — see 'provost help'."
            ;;
    esac
}
main "$@"
